window.ELE_CATALOG=[{"id":"ad-or-info","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":40,"areas":["S2.0","S5.0","S3.0","AI4.0"],"text":"ad or info? students sort mock posts, videos, and headlines into ads, information, and clickbait, hunt for persuasion tricks, and then create an honest, clearly labeled ad of their own. kids see ads everywhere, and many don't look like ads: a favorite gamer's \"honest review,\" a sponsored search result, a cartoon inside a free game, a shocking headline built to get a click. in this lesson teams sort fourteen mock media items into **ad** (wants you to buy), **info** (wants you to know), and **clickbait** (wants you to click), using a persuasion detective checklist. then pairs flip roles and become media makers, creating an honest ad for a made-up product, labeled so no one is fooled. along the way they see that ai can write persuasive text in seconds, which makes spotting persuasion a skill everyone needs. s2.2 s5.2 s3.2 ai4.1 i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i can use creativity and logic to find innovative solutions. creative problem solver i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking media literacy generative ai literacy ad, info, or clickbait? persuasion detective make an honest ad the cereal review three questions and the detective checklist team sort the sneaky ones make an honest ad exit ticket 3-5 copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"after-the-rumor","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","leaders","staff"],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["digcit","medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":60,"areas":["C5.0","AI2.0","T2.0"],"text":"after the rumor a campus team digs past blame to the root causes of a fictional viral rumor and ai-edited image incident, then writes the trust and ai-use norms it will hold itself to next time. when an online rumor tears through a campus, adults tend to respond on their own: some address it in class, some ignore it, someone forwards the image to ask \"is this real?\", and a well-meaning email makes things worse. this protocol uses a fictional incident, an ai-edited photo of a student and a rumor about who made it, to practice a structured, no-blame root-cause analysis. teams trace the problem to causes they can act on, then write two sets of norms: how they'll respond together next time, and how they'll use ai inside their own plc. c5.2 c5.3 c5.1 ai2.1 t2.2 i can facilitate productive discussions and problem-solving sessions. collaborative leadership i am aware of strategies to build trust and promote teamwork. collaborative leadership i know how to foster a collaborative culture among educators. collaborative leadership i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i know how to guide students in safe and responsible internet use. digital literacy digital citizenship media literacy generative ai literacy case file: \"the photo at mesquite hills\" root-cause organizer team norms drafting sheet trust check read the case file five whys, out loud team root-cause analysis circle of control write the norms agree and schedule the check 6-8 9-12 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"ai-ethics-case-court","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","teachers","faculty","coaches"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":70,"areas":["AI2.0","AI1.0"],"text":"ai ethics case court educators put fictional ai uses in grading, proctoring, detection, and monitoring on trial, then issue rulings with conditions that could become real policy. schools and colleges are already making consequential decisions with ai: scoring writing, flagging suspected cheating, monitoring student devices, predicting who might drop out. these uses can save time and catch real problems, and they can also harm the students with the least power to push back. in this mock-court session, small courts argue fictional cases using an ethical principles bench card, then rule \"allowed,\" \"allowed with conditions,\" or \"not allowed.\" the conditions they write become a draft of practical guidance for their own campus. ai2.2 ai2.1 ai2.3 ai1.3 i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship case files ethics bench card ruling and remedy form would you sign it? the bench card prepare the case court is in session compare rulings from verdict to guidance k-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"ai-study-buddy-contract","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":55,"areas":["S4.0","AI2.0","S1.0"],"text":"ai study buddy contract students sort ai uses that build learning from ones that replace it, then write a smart goal, a personal ai contract, and an honest-use log they keep for two weeks. ai can quiz you, explain a tricky idea a new way, or give feedback on a draft. it can also do the thinking for you, so the assignment gets done but nothing gets learned. in this activity, students sort realistic ai uses into \"builds my learning,\" \"replaces my learning,\" and \"depends, check the rules.\" then they compare two teacher-projected ai responses to the same request, set a smart learning goal, and write a personal ai study buddy contract with an honest-use log. the question at the center: did the tool make *me* smarter, or just make the work *look* done? s4.1 s4.2 s4.3 ai2.1 s1.3 i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i can set achievable goals and monitor my progress. self-directed learner i am aware of my learning strengths and areas for improvement. self-directed learner i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of how ai can assist me in my studies. tech savvy generative ai literacy digital citizenship builds it or replaces it? sort my ai study buddy contract honest-use log the gym analogy builds it or replaces it? same topic, two requests know yourself write the contract start the log 6-8 9-12 academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds","type":"activity"},{"id":"algorithm-autopsy","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":75,"areas":["AI1.0","S5.0","S2.0"],"text":"algorithm autopsy students dissect the design features of a fictional social app, trace how each one shapes attention and belief, and redesign the feed for people instead of engagement. recommendation systems are a form of ai most students use every day without choosing to. they predict what will keep a person watching and rank content accordingly, and the app around them is designed with features like autoplay, streaks, and infinite scroll that keep people on the app longer. in this autopsy, teams examine the features of a fictional app called loop, sort each one by what it is optimized to do, and discuss in a socratic seminar whether a feed can shape what people believe. they finish by designing a healthier feed with specific rules, trade-offs, and a way to measure whether it works. ai1.1 s5.2 s5.1 ai1.3 s2.2 i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i can use creativity and logic to find innovative solutions. creative problem solver i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety media literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship loop feature autopsy the loop memo (seminar text) healthier feed design spec why did it show me that? how a recommender works the feature autopsy socratic seminar: can a feed change your mind? design a healthier feed pitch and pledge 9-12 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"ask-two","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":35,"areas":["S4.0","AI4.0","S2.0"],"text":"ask two a confident robot helper says things that are sometimes right and sometimes wrong, and children become learning owners by checking each answer in two places before they believe it. talking speakers, apps, and ai chatbots answer children's questions in a friendly, sure-sounding voice. sounding sure is not the same as being right. in this lesson, **robo helper** (you, reading answer cards, plus an optional projected ai chatbot) gives answers to simple science and everyday questions. pairs pick up a card and **ask two**: they check it at two of three stations (book nook, look and see, grown-up desk) before deciding right, wrong, or not sure yet. children practice owning their learning: asking, checking, and changing their minds when the evidence says so. s4.1 ai4.1 s2.2 s4.3 ai4.2 i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i am aware of my learning strengths and areas for improvement. self-directed learner i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy media literacy robo helper says… my ask two card i am a learning owner robo helper sounds very sure ask two: think aloud checking stations what did robo get wrong? i am a learning owner k-2","type":"activity"},{"id":"bias-detectives","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians","coaches"],"grades":["6-8","9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","medialit","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["AI1.0","AI2.0","AI4.0"],"text":"bias detectives teams compare paired ai outputs where only a name, a language, or a pronoun changed, then run their own fair tests and propose fixes before ai output reaches students. ai models learn from human-made text and images, so they can absorb the stereotypes, gaps, and defaults in that data. bias rarely announces itself; it shows up when you change one small thing and the output shifts. in this session teams act as detectives on printed, clearly fictional mock output pairs (a recommendation letter, a translation, career advice, writing feedback), then design and run their own paired-prompt test on a district-approved tool. they leave with a fair-test protocol and a mitigation plan for one real classroom use. ai1.3 ai2.2 ai4.1 ai2.1 i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use generative ai literacy media literacy digital citizenship mock output case files detective log fair test planner spot the difference work the case files what makes a fair test run your own test patterns and fixes one use, one guardrail 6-8 9-12 higher ed bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"bias-in-the-machine","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":50,"areas":["AI1.0","S3.0","AI2.0","T3.0"],"text":"bias in the machine students read six mock ai outputs through different perspectives, find who is stereotyped or missing, and rewrite the prompts to fix it. generative ai learns from huge amounts of text and images made by people, so it can repeat the patterns in that material, including stereotypes and blind spots. in this activity, teams read six printed mock chatbot responses to everyday prompts. each team member reads through a different perspective lens, so the group notices more together than anyone would alone. teams name the assumption in each output, identify who is missing, and rewrite the prompt or the output so it works for more people. ai1.3 s3.3 ai2.2 s3.2 t3.3 i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i am aware of the value of diverse perspectives in group work. collaborative learner i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i am aware of cultural differences and how they impact learning. inclusive teaching generative ai literacy media literacy mock ai outputs who's missing? organizer perspective lenses and rewrite draw a scientist read through a lens who's missing? rewrite it why it matters, who decides 6-8 9-12 bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"cart-check","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["digcit","genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":55,"areas":["S2.0","S5.0","AI4.0"],"text":"cart check: shopping and money scams online before they tap \"buy,\" students check the storefront, the deal, the reviews, and the payment method, and they practice exactly what to do if a purchase goes wrong. online shopping scams rarely look like scams anymore. a fake storefront can have a polished design, a countdown timer, and hundreds of glowing reviews that an ai tool wrote in minutes. in this activity, students examine a mock storefront, sort real-looking shopping situations into buy, walk away, or check first, and build a four-part cart check: the store, the deal, the reviews, and the payment. they also weigh buy-now-pay-later plans honestly and rehearse the steps to take if they are scammed, because knowing what to do fast matters as much as avoiding the trap. s2.1 s5.1 ai4.1 s2.2 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety digital citizenship generative ai literacy media literacy the kicks vault storefront buy, walk away, or check first? my cart check and scam response plan the deal of the year investigate the storefront think aloud: the four-part cart check sort the situations pay later, pay more? if it goes wrong one check i'll actually use 9-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"circles-not-screens","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":60,"areas":["T4.0","T3.0","L5.0"],"text":"circles, not screens: relationships in a digital classroom teachers sit in a real community circle about group chats, ai companions, and sleep, then plan their own circle, co-create agreements, and rehearse what to say when a student tells them something online went wrong. students' social lives now run through group chats, games, shared photos, and, for some, ai companion chatbots, yet those lives rarely come into the classroom until something goes wrong. a community circle is a simple, device-free structure that makes space to talk about online life before it becomes a crisis. in this session, teachers first sit in a circle as students, then choose and adapt grade-level prompts, turn the circle's needs into agreements, and rehearse the first two minutes after a student discloses a problem online. they leave with a planned first circle and a short note that invites families into the conversation. t4.1 t4.3 t4.2 t3.1 l5.1 i know how to foster positive relationships with my students. relationship building i am aware of the importance of emotional support in academic success. relationship building i can create opportunities for student collaboration and peer learning. relationship building i know how to create a welcoming classroom environment for all students. inclusive teaching i know how to build strong digital partnerships with families and community organizations. technology-enabled community engagement digital citizenship generative ai literacy circle prompt cards when a student tells you my first circle planner open the circle sit in a circle as students choose and adapt prompts for your grade from needs to agreements rehearse: when a student tells you plan your first circle and a family bridge k-12 kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"co-creation-not-substitution","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","faculty","coaches","librarians"],"grades":["6-8","9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":75,"areas":["AI5.0","AI2.0"],"text":"co-creation, not substitution educators sort real classroom and faculty tasks along a human-to-ai spectrum, co-create something with ai while keeping their own thinking visible, and write the disclosure and syllabus statements that make the line clear to students. the useful question isn't \"ai or no ai?\" it's \"who does which part of the thinking, and does that serve the learning goal?\" in this session participants sort classroom and faculty tasks along a spectrum from human-only to ai-assisted to ai-generated with human review, then experience a creative co-creation round where ai widens the options and the human chooses, transforms, and owns the result. they finish by writing a student disclosure statement and a course ai-use statement, with a higher education syllabus variant, so students know exactly where ai belongs in each assignment, if it belongs at all. ai5.1 ai5.3 ai5.2 ai2.3 i know how to foster collaboration between humans and ai systems. ai collaboration and innovation i am aware of the potential of ai to augment human creativity and problem-solving. ai collaboration and innovation i can use ai as a tool for innovation in educational projects and research. ai collaboration and innovation i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship collaboration spectrum sort co-creation log and disclosure builder model ai-use statements: k–12 and higher ed who wrote this? walk the spectrum co-creation round whose idea is it? anatomy of a good disclosure write your statements try it with students 6-8 9-12 higher ed copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"co-plan-in-20","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":50,"areas":["C1.0","C2.0","C4.0","AI4.0"],"text":"co-plan in 20 a timed, listening-first protocol a coach and teacher use to redesign one upcoming lesson so students verify an ai answer or evaluate a source, and turn in proof that they did. most teachers have twenty minutes, not an afternoon, and most coaching conversations drift toward a tool before anyone has named the learning. co-plan in 20 sits in the investigate and innovate phase of the tcea eiir coaching cycle: a five-segment protocol that fits a planning period. the coach listens, uses the arc moves (acknowledge the reality, reframe the problem from a student deficit to a design barrier, commit to one high-leverage shift), redesigns one moment of the lesson, models it, and sets a date to look at student work. participants run it in pairs on a real or persona lesson where students use ai output or online sources, then debrief what kept the conversation on the teacher's goal. c1.1 c2.1 c1.2 c4.2 ai4.1 i know how to mentor teachers in effective instructional practices. teacher support i know how to guide teachers in meaningful technology integration. technology integration i can provide constructive feedback to improve teaching strategies. teacher support i can identify and address teachers' individual learning needs. professional development i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy media literacy role cards the listening-first script co-planning template where did your last planning conversation go? read the protocol fishbowl the first eight minutes run the full protocol what kept it on the teacher's goal? book the real twenty minutes k-12 higher ed copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"coach-to-coach-microteaching","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":90,"areas":["C4.0","C5.0","C1.0","AI4.0"],"text":"coach-to-coach microteaching coaches plan and deliver 10-minute micro-pd sessions on one digital literacy move, get protocol-based peer feedback, and revise before a teacher ever sees it. coaches are often asked to teach a skill to adults in the ten minutes at the start of a staff meeting, and those ten minutes rarely get rehearsed. in this session, each coach draws one digital literacy move (tracing a quote, checking an ai answer's sources, a privacy pause before uploading), plans a 10-minute micro-pd with a rise goal and adult learning principles built in, delivers it to two colleagues, and receives feedback through a structured glow, grow, and try protocol. coaches also test an ai-drafted set of feedback stems and decide what to keep. everyone revises and re-delivers the weakest two minutes before leaving. c4.1 c4.3 c5.3 c1.2 ai4.2 i know how to design and deliver effective professional development sessions. professional development i am aware of adult learning principles and how to apply them. professional development i am aware of strategies to build trust and promote teamwork. collaborative leadership i can provide constructive feedback to improve teaching strategies. teacher support i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking media literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship move cards and ai-drafted stems micro-pd planner micro-pd feedback rubric ten minutes, rehearsed draw a move plan the ten minutes critique the ai-drafted stems three rounds: deliver, feedback, reflect revise and re-deliver two minutes what changed in the revision? book your ten minutes k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds","type":"activity"},{"id":"community-problem-pitch","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":110,"areas":["S5.0","S3.0","AI5.0"],"text":"community problem pitch teams use the engineering design process to solve a real school or community problem, invite ai to critique their ideas, reject what doesn't hold up, and pitch a solution with a record of what changed. students pick a real problem they can observe at school or in their community, gather evidence, and generate their own ideas before any ai is involved. then ai joins as a brainstorm critic: the teacher projects a district-approved chatbot (or uses a printed mock critique) that suggests ideas and pokes holes in the team's plan. some of its suggestions are useful, some are unrealistic, and some include claims no one can verify. teams must decide what to keep, verify, or reject, build a simple prototype, and pitch it. the grade rests on their reasoning and their record of what changed, not on the ai's ideas. s5.1 s5.2 s3.1 s3.2 ai5.3 i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver i can use creativity and logic to find innovative solutions. creative problem solver i know how to work effectively in a team. collaborative learner i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i am aware of the potential of ai to augment human creativity and problem-solving. ai collaboration and innovation generative ai literacy media literacy sample ai critic response design log pitch rubric problem hunt (day 1) define it and gather evidence our ideas first meet the critic (day 2) keep, verify, or reject prototype it the pitch (day 2 or 3) who did the thinking? 6-8 9-12 ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"credit-where-its-due","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":45,"areas":["S1.0","AI2.0","S4.0"],"text":"credit where it's due after their own doodles get \"borrowed\" without credit, students learn to credit creators, ask permission, and write an honest note whenever ai helped with their work. everything students find online was made by someone: a photographer, a writer, a musician, a kid like them. and now some of what students make gets help from ai. in this lesson students feel what it's like when their work is taken without credit, sort situations into **give credit**, **ask permission first**, **say ai helped**, and **my own work**, then practice writing credit lines and **ai help notes** for a fictional student's poster before writing them for their own project. the message is honest, not scary: ai can help you learn, and saying how it helped is part of owning your work. s1.3 ai2.1 ai2.3 s4.1 i am aware of how ai can assist me in my studies. tech savvy i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner digital citizenship generative ai literacy credit sort rosa's volcano poster my credit lines and ai help note the doodle heist credit lines and ai help notes credit sort fix rosa's poster my own credits why credit matters 3-5 copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"data-trail-audit","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":65,"areas":["S2.0","AI2.0"],"text":"data trail audit teams read an ai study app's privacy policy like investigators, map where their data could travel, and choose settings and habits that shrink their trail. every app and ai tool students use collects something: what they type, where they are, how long they look, who they talk to. privacy policies explain much of it, but they are long and written for lawyers. in this audit, teams decode a realistic mock privacy policy for a fictional ai study app, trace how a single data point can travel from the app to partners and data brokers, and then audit a tool they actually use. they leave with a short, evidence-based plan for what to share, what to change in settings, and what to stop typing into chatbots. s2.1 s2.3 ai2.3 ai2.2 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use digital citizenship generative ai literacy studynest ai privacy policy (excerpt) data trail cards policy audit organizer what did you tell the internet today? reading a policy like an investigator audit studynest follow the trail audit a real tool my data plan 9-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"deepfake-election-desk","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":70,"areas":["AI2.0","S3.0","S2.0","AI4.0"],"text":"deepfake election desk student newsroom teams race a deadline to decide which election-week tips are real, which are synthetic, and which they simply can't confirm yet. it is three days before the mayoral election in the fictional city of port alder, and tips are flooding the newsroom of the *port alder ledger*: an audio clip, a rally photo, a video, a screenshot. some are authentic, some are ai-generated or edited, and some cannot be confirmed before deadline. student teams work as a verification desk, using provenance questions, lateral reading, and source contact rather than gut feel or a single detector, and they must write a publish, hold, or debunk decision they can defend. the big idea: with synthetic media, the most responsible answer is sometimes \"we don't know yet,\" and saying so is part of the job. ai2.2 s3.1 s2.2 ai4.1 s3.2 i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i know how to work effectively in a team. collaborative learner i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner media literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship incoming tips (port alder mayoral election) verification desk log editor's decision memo rubric real or made? the verification desk's four questions deadline round the morning meeting the editor's memo 9-12 deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"family-ai-night-design","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","staff","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":90,"areas":["L5.0","L4.0","AI1.0"],"text":"designing a family ai & digital citizenship night campus teams design a family event where parents and kids try things together, check what ai tells them, and go home with conversations to have, not just rules to follow. families are hearing about ai, deepfakes, and screen time from every direction, often in alarming headlines. a family night can replace fear with practice, if it's designed for participation rather than a slideshow in the cafeteria. in this session, campus teams first experience two sample stations as families would, then choose and adapt stations from a menu, plan for the families least likely to attend, line up community partners, and customize a printable take-home that works even for families who never come. the measure of success is what families do at home the following week. l5.1 l5.3 l4.1 ai1.2 i know how to build strong digital partnerships with families and community organizations. technology-enabled community engagement i am aware of strategies to leverage online community resources to support student learning. technology-enabled community engagement i know how to create a positive and inclusive digital learning climate. fostering a digital learning culture i can explain ai concepts in simple terms to various stakeholders. ai literacy digital citizenship generative ai literacy media literacy station menu cards family night planning canvas take-home: five conversations about ai and life online what families are asking be the family explain it to a family design your night customize the take-home share and measure first phone call k-12 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"did-the-ai-help","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","faculty","leaders"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":75,"areas":["AI3.0","AI4.0","AI5.0"],"text":"did the ai help? educators pick apart a fictional \"it worked!\" report, then design a small, honest classroom inquiry that could actually show whether an ai-supported task improved learning. \"the kids loved it\" and \"scores went up\" are the most common evidence offered for ai in classrooms, and neither shows the ai caused the learning. you don't need a research grant to do better. in this session participants sort study designs by what they can honestly conclude, critique a fictional teacher's inquiry report, and then plan a small, fair comparison for their own classroom or course: one learning target, a comparison that's as fair as practical, student work scored without knowing which condition produced it, and a plan to act on whatever they find, including \"it didn't help.\" ai3.2 ai4.3 ai5.2 ai3.1 i can evaluate the effectiveness of ai-enhanced learning interventions. ai-enhanced learning i am aware of strategies to develop ai-related problem-solving skills. ai and critical thinking i can use ai as a tool for innovation in educational projects and research. ai collaboration and innovation i know how to leverage ai tools to personalize and enhance learning experiences. ai-enhanced learning generative ai literacy media literacy can we tell? sort mock inquiry report: ms. delgado's biology classes small honest inquiry planner it worked! or did it? can we tell? critique the mock report four moves for a fairer comparison plan your inquiry evidence that would convince me calendar it k-12 higher ed prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds","type":"activity"},{"id":"digital-footprint-trail","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":40,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0","AI2.0"],"text":"digital footprint trail teams follow a trail of paper footprints left online by a fictional fourth grader, piece together what a stranger could learn about him, and plan the footprints they want to leave. every post, comment, photo, quiz answer, and chatbot message leaves a **digital footprint**, and footprints add up. in this walk-around mystery, teams follow twelve paper footprints left by **kai morales**, a fictional fourth grader. no single post seems like a big deal. put together, they reveal kai's school, street, house number, the hint to his password, and when he's home alone. the trail also includes footprints kai can be proud of. teams then give kai a footprint makeover and plan their own trail, including what they will and won't type into ai tools. s2.3 s2.1 s4.3 ai2.1 i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of my learning strengths and areas for improvement. self-directed learner i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use digital citizenship generative ai literacy kai's footprint cards trail log my footprint plan who walked here? walk the trail what a stranger now knows three p's before you post kai's footprint makeover my footprint plan 3-5 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"digital-notebook-detectives","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":45,"areas":["S1.0","S4.0","AI4.0"],"text":"digital notebook detectives students rescue a classmate's messy pretend drive, invent a naming and folder system, use version history to recover lost work, and catch what an ai summary of their notes left out. students now keep a lot of their schoolwork in digital notebooks and drives, and \"i can't find it\" costs real learning time. in this hybrid lesson, teams open a paper \"drive\" belonging to a fictional student, theo, full of files named *untitled document (2)* and *asdfgh*. they race to find one assignment, then build folders and a naming rule, use a printed version history to recover a paragraph a teammate deleted, and compare theo's field trip notes to an ai summary that sounds complete but drops the most important details. students leave owning a filing plan they can use tomorrow on a real device. s1.1 s4.1 s1.2 ai4.1 i know how to use technology to support my learning. tech savvy i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i can adapt to new digital tools and platforms. tech savvy i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking digital citizenship generative ai literacy theo's messy drive owl report version history and the field trip notes my filing plan find it fast! build theo's folders one naming rule for everyone version history detectives did the ai summary leave something out? my filing plan 3-5 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"disclose-it","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":55,"areas":["AI2.0","S4.0"],"text":"disclose it students sort real-feeling academic integrity dilemmas across courses, then write honest ai-use disclosures and process logs they would stand behind. students increasingly take several courses at once with several different ai rules: brainstorming allowed in one, banned in another, required with a log in a third. the skill that transfers across all of them is not memorizing rules; it is being transparent about what you did and taking ownership of your process. in this activity, students sort fourteen scenarios by what the posted course policy requires, argue the gray areas, and then write a disclosure statement and process log for a piece of their own work. no devices are needed, because the thinking is about judgment and honesty, not tools. ai2.3 ai2.1 s4.1 ai2.2 i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship where does it land? (scenario sort) disclosure statement and process log disclosure quality check same move, different class the scenario sort argue the gray areas vague vs. specific disclosure write your own disclosure and log partner check 9-12 higher ed academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"does-this-need-a-screen","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":20,"areas":["T1.0","T4.0"],"text":"does this need a screen? a fast, argument-starting card sort where teachers decide which classroom tasks are best unplugged, which earn their screen time, and where ai adds value, and have to say why. the screen-time debate usually gets stuck at \"more\" or \"less.\" this quick sort replaces that with a better question: for *this* task and *these* students, what does a screen add to the evidence of learning, and what does it cost in attention, connection, and wellbeing? groups sort 14 real-feeling classroom tasks into four piles, argue the close calls, and leave with a five-question check they can use while planning tomorrow. it works well as a staff-meeting opener or the first 20 minutes of a longer session. t1.1 t4.3 t1.3 i know how to seamlessly integrate technology into my teaching practices. technology integration i am aware of the importance of emotional support in academic success. relationship building i am aware of emerging technologies and their potential applications in education. technology integration digital citizenship screen or no screen? sort five-question screen check stand on the line sort and argue the contested strips check one real task k-12 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"evidence-before-tools","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["T1.0","T5.0","AI3.0"],"text":"evidence before tools teachers take apart a tech-heavy lesson, name the evidence of learning first, and rebuild it so every tool, or no tool, has to earn its place. a lesson can be full of devices, apps, and ai and still produce almost no evidence that anyone learned. participants first *do* a busy, tech-heavy research lesson as students, then audit it tool by tool and ask what thinking each step makes visible or hides. borrowing the ms. ramirez scenario from the 2026 ele update, they rebuild the lesson backward from the evidence they want, then apply the same move to a lesson of their own. the big idea: technology use by itself is not evidence of learning. t1.1 t1.2 t5.1 ai3.2 i know how to seamlessly integrate technology into my teaching practices. technology integration i can use ai tools to enhance student learning experiences. technology integration i know how to design lessons that promote higher-order thinking skills. critical thinking i can evaluate the effectiveness of ai-enhanced learning interventions. ai-enhanced learning media literacy generative ai literacy the \"before\" lesson: pollinator power tool-to-evidence audit evidence-first redesign planner two rooms, one question be the students: the before lesson think aloud: audit the first two steps teams finish the audit and rebuild the lesson your own lesson, evidence first what earned its screen time? k-12 data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"explain-it-to-grandma","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians","coaches","leaders"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["AI1.0"],"text":"explain it to grandma educators draft 90-second, jargon-free explanations of ai concepts and test them live on colleagues playing a skeptical grandparent, a second grader, or a school board trustee. families, students, and board members are forming opinions about ai in schools right now, often from headlines. the educators who can explain it plainly and honestly, without hype or fear, shape that conversation. in this session participants pair an audience card with a concept card, draft a 90-second explanation, and then test it on a colleague who plays that audience and pushes back with the audience's real questions. explanations get scored for plain language and for accuracy, because simple is only useful if it's also true. ai1.2 ai1.1 ai1.3 i can explain ai concepts in simple terms to various stakeholders. ai literacy i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship audience and concept cards 90-second explanation planner plain and accurate rubric the jargon wall watch one, then critique it draw, draft, trim role-play rounds words that worked where will you use it? k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"feedback-partners-ai-drafts","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","coaches","leaders"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["T4.0","T5.0","T3.0","L2.0"],"text":"feedback partners: peer review in the age of ai teachers run a glow-grow-question peer review on two ai-assisted drafts, questioning the ai's suggestions as hard as the writer's, then adapt the protocol for their own students. when students revise with a generative ai tool, the tool's suggestions can quietly replace the most valuable feedback a writer gets: a real reader's question. this session puts peers back at the center. participants act as students and give each other glow, grow, and question feedback on two fictional ai-assisted drafts. they also judge each ai suggestion (accept, adapt, or reject) and have to explain why. then they debrief as teachers and adapt the protocol, with supports, for their own grade level. they finish by naming what a coach or leader would see in a classroom where this is working. t4.2 t5.2 t5.1 t3.2 l2.1 i can create opportunities for student collaboration and peer learning. relationship building i can use questioning techniques to stimulate deep learning. critical thinking i know how to design lessons that promote higher-order thinking skills. critical thinking i can adapt my teaching methods to accommodate diverse learning needs. inclusive teaching i know how to support and evaluate effective technology integration practices. technology-enhanced instructional leadership generative ai literacy digital citizenship two ai-assisted drafts feedback partner log protocol adapter feedback you remember fishbowl the protocol partners as students: draft 2 what did the peer catch that the ai didn't? adapt the protocol for your class what would a visitor see? k-12 higher ed academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds","type":"activity"},{"id":"four-lens-app-review","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","librarians","leaders"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["C2.0","AI2.0","T3.0"],"text":"four-lens app review teams vet three fictional classroom apps through four lenses (learning value, privacy, accessibility, ai transparency) and turn the verdict into a pilot a teacher can actually run. teachers forward coaches a steady stream of \"can we use this?\" messages, and more of the tools now include ai features that write, grade, or talk to students. in this protocol, teams review fictional app profiles and policy excerpts through four lenses, cite the exact line behind every score, and practice reading vendor claims laterally rather than taking them at face value. the session ends with a decision and a pilot plan built around the 2026 ele question: what student evidence will show the tool improved the learning? c2.2 ai2.3 c2.1 t3.2 i can evaluate the effectiveness of educational technology tools. technology integration i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i know how to guide teachers in meaningful technology integration. technology integration i can adapt my teaching methods to accommodate diverse learning needs. inclusive teaching digital citizenship generative ai literacy app profiles: three fictional tools four-lens rubric recommendation and pilot plan the email in your inbox first review: one app, four lenses reading a vendor claim laterally second review and the room grid decide and design the pilot what we'd tell the teacher k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"platform-pick-to-community-story","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","staff"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":90,"areas":["L2.0","L3.0","L5.0"],"text":"from platform pick to community story a selection committee weighs three fictional math platforms on evidence, assessment data, accessibility, and privacy, then tells families the honest story of the choice in plain language, in more than one language, with a real way to talk back. choosing a digital curriculum platform is only half the job. the other half is explaining the choice to families who will see it on their child's screen every night. in this session, committees in fictional laurel ridge isd compare three fictional grades 3–8 math platforms using the evidence behind their claims, the kind of assessment data they produce, accessibility, privacy, and ai features. they read a small local pilot honestly, choose, and then replace an overhyped ai-drafted announcement with a plain-language communication plan (newsletter blurb, family faq, social post) that says what they know, what they don't know yet, and how families can weigh in, in the district's main home languages. l2.2 l3.3 l3.2 l5.2 l5.1 i can guide the implementation of digital curriculum and instructional technology initiatives. technology-enhanced instructional leadership i am aware of various digital assessment tools and their appropriate uses in education. data-informed decision making with digital tools i can interpret complex digital data sets and communicate findings effectively using data visualization tools. data-informed decision making with digital tools i can effectively use digital communication tools to share school goals and achievements with stakeholders. technology-enabled community engagement i know how to build strong digital partnerships with families and community organizations. technology-enabled community engagement digital citizenship generative ai literacy media literacy the laurel ridge platform packet selection matrix community communication plan the announcement that wrote itself read the packet in role fill the selection matrix decide, and read the pilot honestly write the community story the kitchen-table read the five questions, one more time k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"ai-guidance-from-values","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","faculty","coaches"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":75,"areas":["L1.0","AI2.0","L4.0"],"text":"from values to guidance: drafting a campus ai statement leadership teams start with what they value, sort draft clauses, and write a one-page generative ai guidance statement that people can actually follow. many campuses have ai guidance that is either a ban nobody enforces or a list of tools nobody reads. this session builds guidance the other way around: teams choose the values that matter most for learning on their campus, sort a deck of draft clauses to find which ones are clear, fair, and enforceable, and then draft a one-page statement covering acceptable use, disclosure, privacy, and equity. along the way they critique an ai-generated draft, which models the same judgment the guidance asks of students and staff. l1.1 ai2.1 ai2.3 l4.1 i know how to create and communicate a compelling vision for technology integration in the school. visionary technology leadership i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i know how to create a positive and inclusive digital learning climate. fostering a digital learning culture generative ai literacy digital citizenship values cards clause sort guidance statement drafting frame would anyone know what to do? values card sort sort the clauses critique an ai-written draft draft the statement gallery walk and stress test who sees it next? k-12 higher ed deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"garbage-in-garbage-out","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":40,"areas":["AI1.0","S5.0","S3.0","AI4.0"],"text":"garbage in, garbage out students write instructions for a classmate \"robot\" who follows them exactly, discover why vague prompts get surprising results, and build a recipe for clear prompts they can use with ai and with people. computer scientists have an old saying: **garbage in, garbage out.** if the instructions are unclear, the result will be too. in this game, a director secretly draws a picture from a shape challenge and writes instructions, and a classmate robot follows those words exactly, with no peeking and no questions. round 1 results are often wildly off. round 2, after the class builds a \"prompt recipe,\" gets much closer. students then upgrade vague ai prompts and learn the other half of the truth: even a great prompt can get a wrong answer, so people still check. ai1.1 s5.1 s3.2 s5.3 ai4.3 i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i am aware of how to apply computational thinking in various subjects. creative problem solver i am aware of strategies to develop ai-related problem-solving skills. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy shape challenge cards robot instruction sheet prompt upgrade lab program the teacher how ai fills in the gaps round 1 and round 2 build the prompt recipe prompt upgrade lab exit ticket 3-5 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"hallucination-hunt","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["AI4.0","S2.0","AI1.0"],"text":"hallucination hunt students trace every claim and citation in a polished ai research brief and discover which ones lead somewhere and which ones lead nowhere. generative ai tools can produce research summaries that look finished: confident prose, tidy numbers, a reference list in perfect format. some of those references are real, some are real but misrepresented, and some do not exist at all, because the model predicts what a citation *should look like* rather than retrieving one. in this hunt, students audit a mock ai brief about a fictional town's heat problem against a \"library shelf\" of source cards, sort each claim by what the evidence shows, and then repair the brief. every source in the packet is fictional, so the only way to win is to trace, not to recognize. ai4.1 ai4.2 s2.2 ai1.3 i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy generative ai literacy media literacy the ai research brief the library shelf (source cards) claim trace log would you turn this in? why ai invents citations round 1: the reference desk hunt score the brief round 2: hunt a live output the auditor's note 9-12 higher ed copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"headlines-from-year-three","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","coaches"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":70,"areas":["L1.0","L4.0","AI2.0","C5.0"],"text":"headlines from year three leaders write the success story and the failure story of their own ai or tech initiative three years out, then run a pre-mortem on the failure so they can build guardrails before launch. most initiative plans describe only the future where everything works. in this session, leadership teams write two headlines about their own campus ai or technology initiative, dated three years from now: one where they got it right and one where it went wrong. then they run a pre-mortem, the technique psychologist gary klein developed: assume the failure headline came true and explain why. teams sort the reasons through tcea's five-question leadership check and turn the biggest risks into guardrails with owners and check dates, before the initiative has a chance to fail. l1.1 l1.2 l4.2 ai2.1 c5.2 i know how to create and communicate a compelling vision for technology integration in the school. visionary technology leadership i can develop strategic technology plans that align with educational trends and community needs. visionary technology leadership i can develop policies that support equitable access to technology and digital resources. fostering a digital learning culture i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i can facilitate productive discussions and problem-solving sessions. collaborative leadership generative ai literacy digital citizenship worked example: pecan creek isd pre-mortem risk map two headlines and a guardrail plan two stories about the same plan write both headlines the pre-mortem map the risks to the five questions draft the guardrails the stranger test put the check date on the calendar k-12 higher ed algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"human-ai-studio","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":90,"areas":["AI5.0","S5.0","AI2.0"],"text":"human + ai studio students create a short piece with ai as a prompt partner, log every suggestion they keep, change, or reject, and defend their human choices in a studio critique. generative ai can produce a poem, a story opening, or a poster concept in seconds, which raises a real question for student creators: what part of the work is theirs? in this studio, students make a short creative piece (flash fiction of 150–250 words or a one-page design concept) using ai only as a prompt partner that offers ideas, never as the author. they keep a human choices ledger of every ai suggestion and their decision about it, then defend their choices in a studio critique where peers ask, \"why that?\" the evidence of learning is not the finished piece; it's the trail of decisions that made it. ai5.1 ai5.3 s5.2 ai2.3 i know how to foster collaboration between humans and ai systems. ai collaboration and innovation i am aware of the potential of ai to augment human creativity and problem-solving. ai collaboration and innovation i can use creativity and logic to find innovative solutions. creative problem solver i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship ai suggestion deck human choices ledger studio critique rubric spot the choice prompt partner, not ghostwriter studio time studio critique: \"why that?\" revise and disclose 9-12 higher ed academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds","type":"activity"},{"id":"hype-check-showcase","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"digital","minutes":75,"areas":["C2.0","T1.0","AI1.0"],"text":"hype check showcase a tech showcase remix: teachers rotate through emerging-tech stations, including ai and synthetic media, and have to separate the vendor's claim from the learning evidence before they give a verdict. traditional tech showcases are tool petting zoos: people leave excited about a gadget and unsure what students would do with it. this remix keeps the energy and adds a hype check at every station. groups read a claim, try the technology (or examine a printed stand-in), and must record what learning evidence it could produce, what could go wrong, and who might be left out. two stations involve synthetic media, so teachers practice the detection-and-context moves their students need. c2.3 c2.1 t1.3 ai1.3 i am aware of emerging technologies and their potential in education. technology integration i know how to guide teachers in meaningful technology integration. technology integration i am aware of emerging technologies and their potential applications in education. technology integration i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy media literacy generative ai literacy station cards hype check log showcase remix planner which one is really me? one hype check, out loud station rotation room verdicts remix your own showcase one follow-up k-12 higher ed deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"inbox-to-impact","audience":"pl","roles":["staff","teachers","leaders"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":60,"areas":["T4.0","L5.0","AI2.0","T3.0"],"text":"inbox to impact: professional digital communication staff revise five realistic messages to families and colleagues for clarity, kindness, privacy, accessibility, and translation, and learn to review an ai-drafted message before it goes out. a single email can build trust with a family or undo a month of it. in this session, participants work as editors on five fictional messages: a sharp reply to a parent, a staff email that exposes student information, a jargon-heavy newsletter for a multilingual community, an ai-drafted message with a made-up policy in it, and a video-meeting invite missing everything people need. using a shared before you hit send checklist, tables revise each message, then write one real message of their own to use this week. the big idea: professional communication is relationship work, and privacy, access, and ai review are part of being kind. t4.1 l5.2 ai2.1 t3.1 i know how to foster positive relationships with my students. relationship building i can effectively use digital communication tools to share school goals and achievements with stakeholders. technology-enabled community engagement i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i know how to create a welcoming classroom environment for all students. inclusive teaching digital citizenship generative ai literacy five messages that need a second look before you hit send message makeover log read it as the family would think aloud with the checklist editing stations what never goes in an email? video-meeting norms in two minutes one real message our team's message norms k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"digital-learning-rounds","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","coaches","teachers"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":75,"areas":["L2.0","L3.0","T4.0"],"text":"instructional rounds for digital learning leadership teams run a full rounds protocol on five written classroom vignettes, noting only what students do with technology and ai, then find patterns and name the next level of work for teachers, coaches, and themselves. walkthroughs often end in a verdict (\"great tech use!\") that tells a teacher nothing and tells a campus even less. instructional rounds work differently: a team agrees on a problem of practice, gathers descriptive evidence of what students are actually doing, looks for patterns across classrooms, and names the next level of work for the whole system, not for one teacher. in this session, teams practice the protocol on five written vignettes from a fictional middle school whose problem of practice is about students reasoning together with technology and ai. they set their notes beside the campus walkthrough app's summary to see what each kind of data can and can't tell them. unlike building a look-for rubric, rounds are about learning to see and deciding together what the campus does next. l2.1 l2.3 l2.2 l3.1 t4.2 i know how to support and evaluate effective technology integration practices. technology-enhanced instructional leadership i am aware of research-based approaches to technology-enhanced pedagogy and their applications. technology-enhanced instructional leadership i can guide the implementation of digital curriculum and instructional technology initiatives. technology-enhanced instructional leadership i know how to use digital data tools to drive school improvement efforts. data-informed decision making with digital tools i can create opportunities for student collaboration and peer learning. relationship building generative ai literacy media literacy coyote run middle school: problem of practice and five classrooms describe, don't judge sort patterns and next level of work what did you actually see? describe, don't judge the problem of practice and a model note rounds: visit four classrooms patterns, then the walkthrough data next level of work your own problem of practice k-12 ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"deepfake-principal-tabletop","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","staff"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":70,"areas":["L5.0","L4.0","AI4.0","AI1.0"],"text":"is that really our principal? a synthetic media crisis tabletop a fake audio clip of the principal spreads through parent groups on a friday night, and the leadership team has two hours of game time to verify, respond, and rebuild trust. voice-cloning and video tools make it possible to put convincing words in anyone's mouth, and school leaders are visible, recorded, and trusted, which makes them targets. in this tabletop, a crisis team works through a fictional friday-night timeline in which a synthetic audio clip of the principal cancels a trip and insults a neighborhood. teams must separate what they know from what they don't, verify without relying on a detector, communicate with families in the first hour, and plan how to help the whole community get better at spotting the next one. the deepest lesson is that verification is a practice you prepare before the crisis, not a tool you find during it. l5.2 l4.1 ai4.2 ai1.2 i can effectively use digital communication tools to share school goals and achievements with stakeholders. technology-enabled community engagement i know how to create a positive and inclusive digital learning climate. fostering a digital learning culture i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking i can explain ai concepts in simple terms to various stakeholders. ai literacy media literacy generative ai literacy incident timeline cards verification and response log synthetic media readiness checklist would you believe it? play the timeline read the first messages aloud verification without a detector readiness audit the pre-bunk k-12 deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"is-this-health-advice-real","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["S2.0","AI4.0","S5.0"],"text":"is this health advice real? students check influencer clips, supplement ads, forum posts, and a chatbot answer about wellness by reading laterally, then decide when a question belongs with a trusted adult or clinician. teens meet health and wellness claims everywhere: a fitness influencer's morning routine, a sponsored supplement ad, a forum thread at 1 a.m., a chatbot that answers any question in a confident paragraph. in this activity, teams sort eight clearly fictional health posts by who is behind them, what they want, and what better sources say, using lateral reading instead of gut feeling. the goal is not to decide what is medically true. it is to build a repeatable habit for checking health information and to know when the right next step is a person, not another search. s2.2 ai4.1 s5.1 i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver media literacy generative ai literacy health post cards health claim check log my health info game plan would you try it? round 1: judge it from the post alone think aloud: three questions for health info round 2: read laterally, including the chatbot what flipped, and what belongs with a person? my game plan exit ticket 9-12 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"journal-club-by-design","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","librarians","leaders"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["medialit","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":90,"areas":["C4.0","C1.0"],"text":"journal club by design coaches experience a research journal club as learners, reading a practice article with a skeptic's eye, then design their own pd series around real teacher needs and adult learning principles. a journal club is one of the simplest ways to keep a staff connected to research, and one of the easiest to run badly: an article nobody chose, a summary nobody needed, and no change on monday. coaches first take part in a journal club on a fictional practice article about teaching students to pause before sharing online, practicing the same claim-checking they want teachers to teach. then they step back, name the adult learning principles that made it work or fail, read a set of teacher needs profiles, and design a journal club series built on what their teachers actually need. c4.1 c4.2 c4.3 c1.3 i know how to design and deliver effective professional development sessions. professional development i can identify and address teachers' individual learning needs. professional development i am aware of adult learning principles and how to apply them. professional development i am aware of the latest educational research and best practices. teacher support media literacy digital citizenship practice article: \"pause before you post\" principle and teacher profile cards journal club series planner the best and worst pd you've sat through journal club, as learners step out of the learner seat read the needs, not just the requests design your series tuning protocol the first message k-12 higher ed misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations","type":"activity"},{"id":"kind-by-design","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["3-5","6-8","9-12"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":50,"areas":["T4.0","S3.0"],"text":"kind by design teachers rewrite prickly class messages, co-create norms for digital spaces the way students would, and work through what to do when a student's closest confidant is an ai companion chatbot. relationships don't pause when learning moves online. tone disappears in text, group chats and discussion boards can turn sharp fast, and some students now turn to ai \"companion\" chatbots for comfort and conversation. in this unplugged session, teachers first do what students would do: rewrite real-feeling class messages so they're kind *and* clear, and build a norms charter together. then they work through four scenarios about students and companion chatbots, focusing on what the student needs, what a chatbot can and can't give, and the teacher move that keeps a human relationship at the center. t4.1 t4.3 t4.2 s3.2 i know how to foster positive relationships with my students. relationship building i am aware of the importance of emotional support in academic success. relationship building i can create opportunities for student collaboration and peer learning. relationship building i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner digital citizenship message makeover cards companion chatbot scenarios our digital norms charter same words, three tones message makeover, as students co-create the norms charter when the confidant is a chatbot step out of role plan the first 15 minutes 3-5 6-8 9-12 kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"kind-clicks","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":30,"areas":["S3.0","S2.0","S4.0"],"text":"kind clicks children sort online messages into kind clicks and stop-and-tell moments, turn unkind words into kind ones, and practice asking a trusted grown-up for help. many young children already send hearts, stickers, and messages in games, class apps, and video chats with family. words on a screen still land on a real person. in this lesson children watch a paper heart get crumpled by unkind words, sort messages into **kind click** (a heart made with their hands) and **stop and tell** (a stop hand), rewrite unkind messages into kind ones, and rehearse the exact words to use with a trusted grown-up. messages that celebrate differences are part of the kind set, so children also practice welcoming friends who like different things. s3.2 s2.3 s3.3 s4.1 i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i am aware of the value of diverse perspectives in group work. collaborative learner i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner digital citizenship kind click or stop and tell? fix-it cards my kind click the wrinkled heart two signals sort the messages fix-it and tell-a-grown-up my kind click k-2 scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"lateral-reading-relay","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians","coaches"],"grades":["6-8","9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["T2.0","AI4.0"],"text":"lateral reading relay teams race to verify viral claims and an ai-written summary by leaving the page, not staring harder at it. most of us were taught to judge a source by reading it closely: the about page, the spelling, the look of the site. professional fact-checkers do the opposite. they leave the page and read *laterally*, checking what others say about the source before trusting it. in this relay, educators experience both approaches on a set of claims, including one confident ai-generated summary, and then redesign a lesson so students practice the move that works. t2.1 t2.3 ai4.2 ai4.1 i can teach students to critically evaluate online information. digital literacy i am aware of strategies to help students identify and combat misinformation. digital literacy i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking media literacy generative ai literacy claim cards sift relay log lesson retrofit planner gut check in 60 seconds round 1: read vertically think aloud: the four moves round 2: the relay what changed your mind? retrofit one lesson 6-8 9-12 higher ed misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"lms-tidy-up","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","coaches","librarians"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["T3.0","C2.0","T1.0","S1.0"],"text":"lms tidy-up: courses students can find their way through educators run a student-eye findability test on a messy mock course, then tidy their own with consistent modules, one home for due dates, accessible files, and a check on any ai-generated materials. when students can't find the assignment, the rubric, or the due date, the course has become a scavenger hunt, and the students who struggle most with organization, language, or access pay the highest price. in this session, participants play students trying to complete five everyday tasks in a deliberately messy mock course, counting clicks and dead ends. they then tidy it using a shared checklist: consistent module structure, predictable names, one place for due dates, accessible files, and fewer clicks. finally they check an ai-generated course page before posting and plan a findability test with real students in their own course. t3.2 c2.1 t1.1 s1.2 i can adapt my teaching methods to accommodate diverse learning needs. inclusive teaching i know how to guide teachers in meaningful technology integration. technology integration i know how to seamlessly integrate technology into my teaching practices. technology integration i can adapt to new digital tools and platforms. tech savvy digital citizenship generative ai literacy ms. varga's course, as students see it findability test course tidy-up checklist where's the rubric? findability test as a student what made it hard, and for whom? think aloud: one module, tidied check the ai-generated page tidy your own course plan a real student findability test k-12 higher ed how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"look-fors-for-learning","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","coaches"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":90,"areas":["L2.0","AI3.0","AI4.0"],"text":"look-fors that look for learning leaders sort walkthrough notes, practice on three classroom vignettes, and rebuild their technology observation tool so it captures evidence of learning instead of counting devices. many walkthrough forms still ask \"are students using technology?\" and a yes feels like success. the updated eles say plainly that technology use by itself is not evidence of learning. in this session, leaders sort real-sounding walkthrough notes into evidence of learning versus evidence of device use, calibrate on three classroom vignettes (including one with generative ai), and revise a starter observation rubric so it asks what students are thinking, verifying, and deciding. they leave with a tool they can pilot in next week's walkthroughs. l2.1 l2.3 ai3.2 ai4.1 i know how to support and evaluate effective technology integration practices. technology-enhanced instructional leadership i am aware of research-based approaches to technology-enhanced pedagogy and their applications. technology-enhanced instructional leadership i can evaluate the effectiveness of ai-enhanced learning interventions. ai-enhanced learning i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking media literacy generative ai literacy walkthrough notes sort three classroom vignettes starter look-for rubric what does your form count? sort the notes the two-minute student conversation calibrate on three vignettes rebuild the rubric stress test with a colleague's eye pilot plan k-12 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"looking-for-the-checking","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","librarians"],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["C3.0","AI4.0","T2.0"],"text":"looking for the checking a looking-at-student-work protocol that asks one question of a class set: did students actually verify what the ai told them, and how can we tell? teachers now ask students to \"check what the ai says,\" and most students write \"i checked it.\" this protocol treats that assignment as data. a plc reads a fictional set of eighth-grade research notes, describes what is actually on the page, places each sample on a verification evidence continuum, and plans the instruction the pattern calls for. the move for coaches: shift the conversation from \"are students cheating with ai?\" to \"what does our students' work show about how they verify, and what do we teach next?\" c3.1 c3.2 ai4.2 t2.3 i know how to use data to inform instructional decisions. data-driven coaching i can help teachers interpret and act on assessment results. data-driven coaching i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking i am aware of strategies to help students identify and combat misinformation. digital literacy generative ai literacy media literacy student work set: dust bowl research notes verification evidence continuum protocol record \"i checked it.\" describe only place each sample on the continuum what does the pattern say about our teaching? plan the next move for each group next data date 6-8 9-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works","type":"activity"},{"id":"minutes-with-a-purpose","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","coaches","teachers"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":70,"areas":["L3.0","L4.0","L1.0","C3.0"],"text":"minutes with a purpose: a screen time audit teams audit a fictional district's school-device minutes by purpose, not just by total, and draft grade-band guidance that protects unplugged time and asks for evidence of learning. texas hb 1481 (2025) settled the personal-phone question for the school day, and it explicitly leaves out devices the district provides. how much and how well students use *school-issued* devices is still your district's call. in this audit, teams read a fictional district's device-use snapshot critically, sort real-feeling classroom uses by purpose (creating, researching and collaborating, practicing with feedback, or just watching), and apply the 2026 ele question: \"what evidence shows the technology improved the learning?\" they leave with draft grade-band guidance and an honest, privacy-safe plan to gather their own local data. the goal is intentional, age-appropriate use judged by learning, not a blanket cut. l3.2 l4.3 l3.1 l1.1 c3.1 i can interpret complex digital data sets and communicate findings effectively using data visualization tools. data-informed decision making with digital tools i am aware of strategies to address digital divides and promote responsible technology use. fostering a digital learning culture i know how to use digital data tools to drive school improvement efforts. data-informed decision making with digital tools i know how to create and communicate a compelling vision for technology integration in the school. visionary technology leadership i know how to use data to inform instructional decisions. data-driven coaching digital citizenship media literacy pecan creek isd device-use snapshot (fictional) purpose sort grade-band guidance drafter one number, three reactions read the snapshot like a skeptic think aloud: two strips that fool the report sort, then ask the evidence question draft grade-band guidance plan to gather your own data, honestly the five-question check k-12 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"my-digital-safety-plan","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["S2.0","S1.0","S4.0","T2.0"],"text":"my digital safety plan students work through real-world sharing dilemmas, audit account security and privacy habits, and write a personal safety plan they explain to a family member. staying safe online isn't one rule; it's a set of habits: strong, unique passphrases, two-step login, careful privacy settings, thinking before posting, and knowing what not to type into an ai chatbot. students discuss eight \"what would you do?\" scenarios, audit their own habits with a checklist (no one has to reveal any account details), and write a personal digital safety plan. students who don't have accounts yet plan for a family device, a school account, or their first account. s2.1 s2.3 s1.2 s4.1 t2.2 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i can adapt to new digital tools and platforms. tech savvy i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i know how to guide students in safe and responsible internet use. digital literacy digital citizenship generative ai literacy what would you do? scenario cards safety habits audit my digital safety plan private, personal, or public? what would you do? settings tour safety habits audit write the plan explain it to someone 6-8 9-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds","type":"activity"},{"id":"never-paste-this","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","leaders","staff"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":25,"areas":["C3.0","AI2.0"],"text":"never paste this a fast sort and a de-identification drill so data teams know exactly what student information must never go into an ai tool, and how to ask the question they need without it. data teams are the place where the temptation is strongest: a spreadsheet of scores, a chatbot that can \"find the patterns,\" and fifteen minutes left in the meeting. this short opener, sized for the start of a plc or data meeting, has teams sort realistic data items into three categories, then rewrite a set of raw data-team notes so the instructional question survives and the students become unrecognizable. teams leave with a one-page \"before you paste\" check they can tape to the wall. c3.3 ai2.1 c3.1 i am aware of various data collection and analysis tools. data-driven coaching i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i know how to use data to inform instructional decisions. data-driven coaching digital citizenship generative ai literacy what goes in? sort raw data-team notes before you paste checklist the fifteen-minute temptation sort: what goes in? check the key, argue the middle de-identify the notes tape it to the wall k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"news-from-the-future-circle","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2","3-5"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":30,"areas":["S5.0","AI2.0","S2.0","AI1.0"],"text":"news from the future circle young learners hear friendly pretend news about helpers that might exist someday, draw or act out one helpful thing and one tricky thing, and vote on a class rule for the helper. children are already growing up with talking speakers, apps that answer questions, and toys that chat. in this circle, the teacher reads short, friendly pretend news stories from the future, like a robot helper at the library or a pen pal that is an ai. for each one, children find one helpful thing and one tricky thing, act it out or draw it, and then vote on a class rule for the helper. the big idea: new helpers can be great, and we still get to think, check, and make rules about them. s5.2 ai2.2 s2.3 ai1.3 i can use creativity and logic to find innovative solutions. creative problem solver i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship pretend news cards helpful and tricky family connection: news from the future pretend news from the future helpful thumbs, tricky thumbs act it out or draw it vote on a class rule share and send home k-2 3-5 data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"next-word-by-hand","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5","6-8"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["AI1.0","S5.0","S1.0","AI4.0"],"text":"next word, by hand students build a paper \"word-guessing machine\" from sentence cards, watch it invent a confident false fact about a lake, and find out why a longer prompt helps but never makes a chatbot know things. a generative ai chatbot writes by guessing what word is likely to come next, based on patterns in the writing it learned from. in this game, teams become that machine. they tally which word follows which in eight short sentence cards, then \"write\" new sentences by drawing slips from cups. the sentences come out grammatical and often silly. then, in the **lake round**, the machine produces a sentence that sounds like a fact and is false, even though every piece of it came from true cards. students test whether a longer prompt fixes the problem and leave able to say, in their own words, why a chatbot can sound sure and still be wrong. ai1.1 ai1.3 s5.3 s1.3 ai4.1 i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i am aware of how to apply computational thinking in various subjects. creative problem solver i am aware of how ai can assist me in my studies. tech savvy i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy sentence cards next-word tally sheet how did our machine write that? finish my sentence tally one card together train the machine generate! the lake round does a longer prompt fix it? paper vs. real chatbots, honestly 3-5 6-8 ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"norms-for-the-ai-era","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","leaders"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":50,"areas":["C5.0","C4.0","AI2.0"],"text":"norms for the ai era a plc builds trust first, then sorts real shared-planning dilemmas and drafts its own norms for ai use: disclose it, check it, and never put student data in a prompt. shared planning folders now hold quizzes, rubrics, and parent letters that a chatbot helped draft, and teams rarely talk about it. one teammate feels uneasy using an unmarked ai-written quiz; another quietly pasted student writing into a tool to get feedback ideas. this session gives a plc a structured way to talk about it before it becomes a conflict: a hopes, hesitations, and needs round built on three kinds of trust, a sort of realistic dilemmas, and a one-page set of team norms on disclosure, verification, and privacy that the team agrees to revisit. c5.1 c5.3 c4.2 ai2.1 ai2.3 i know how to foster a collaborative culture among educators. collaborative leadership i am aware of strategies to build trust and promote teamwork. collaborative leadership i can identify and address teachers' individual learning needs. professional development i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship trust protocol cards shared planning dilemmas sort our team's ai norms who made this quiz? hopes, hesitations, and needs sort the dilemmas draft the norms test the norms one word, and a win k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"password-power","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":40,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0","AI2.0"],"text":"password power & secret keepers students rank passwords from weak to strong, build silly-but-strong practice passphrases, and sort tricky moments into keep it secret, share with a trusted grown-up, or tell a grown-up now. by third grade, many students have school logins, game accounts, or a family tablet code. this lesson gives them three habits to protect those accounts. first, teams line up password cards from weakest to strongest and discover that long, random **passphrases** beat short passwords full of personal details. next, they build silly practice passphrases from word cards (never their real ones). then they become **secret keepers**, sorting tricky moments, including a message that asks for a password and a phone call from a voice that sounds just like a relative, into *keep it secret*, *share only with a trusted grown-up*, or *tell a grown-up now*. they leave with a plan for what to do when an account acts weird. s2.1 s2.3 s4.1 ai2.2 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use digital citizenship generative ai literacy password line-up secret keeper sort my password power plan the locked diary password line-up build a practice passphrase secret keeper sort when an account acts weird secret keeper pledge 3-5 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"pd-menu-makeover","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","leaders","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":75,"areas":["C4.0","AI1.0"],"text":"pd menu makeover coaches take apart a one-size-fits-all ai pd day and rebuild it as a choice menu, grounded in a real needs assessment, adult learning principles, and teaching strategies that already work. many campuses have lived through the ai pd day: a keynote about the future, a demo of eight tools, everyone creates an account, and nothing changes on monday. in this design session, coaches analyze a fictional district's original agenda and the comments teachers left afterward, sort a draft needs assessment into questions worth keeping, rewriting, or cutting, and then rebuild the day as a menu. every menu option has to name who it is for, the adult learning principle it honors, the research-based teaching strategy it serves, and the evidence teachers will bring back. the menu includes paths for skeptics, for privacy, and for deciding not to use ai. c4.1 c4.2 c4.3 ai1.2 i know how to design and deliver effective professional development sessions. professional development i can identify and address teachers' individual learning needs. professional development i am aware of adult learning principles and how to apply them. professional development i can explain ai concepts in simple terms to various stakeholders. ai literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship the juniper flats ai day needs assessment sort menu builder the pd day you remember diagnose the agenda sort the needs assessment build the menu persona check what will we measure? your next pd day k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"study-coach-with-guardrails","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"digital","minutes":90,"areas":["AI3.0","S4.0","AI4.0"],"text":"personal learning coach, with guardrails students write custom instructions that turn an ai chatbot into a coach that makes them think, then stress-test it and judge it by evidence, not vibes. an ai chatbot will happily do your homework, and that is exactly the problem: if it does the thinking, you don't learn. but the same tool, given clear instructions, can ask questions, give hints in steps, quiz you, and refuse to hand over answers. in this workshop, students design custom instructions for a study coach tailored to their own goals and learning gaps, test it with tricky \"break it\" prompts, and evaluate whether it actually helped them learn, using evidence they collect. the goal is ownership: students decide how ai fits their learning, and can show whether it does. ai3.1 s4.2 s4.3 ai3.2 ai4.3 i know how to leverage ai tools to personalize and enhance learning experiences. ai-enhanced learning i can set achievable goals and monitor my progress. self-directed learner i am aware of my learning strengths and areas for improvement. self-directed learner i can evaluate the effectiveness of ai-enhanced learning interventions. ai-enhanced learning i am aware of strategies to develop ai-related problem-solving skills. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy two coaches, one question coach design worksheet coach test log two coaches, one question know your goal, your strength, your gap anatomy of good custom instructions write your coach break it: the stress test coach session and after check evidence verdict 9-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"personalization-with-guardrails","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","coaches","librarians"],"grades":["3-5","6-8","9-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":70,"areas":["AI3.0","AI4.0","AI2.0"],"text":"personalization with guardrails teachers design ai-supported versions of one reading task for different learners, then judge every version against the same learning target, a fact check, and a privacy line. ai tools can quickly produce leveled texts, bilingual glossaries, chunked questions, and read-aloud audio, and paired with long-standing assistive technology like text-to-speech and speech-to-text, they can open grade-level content to more students. they can also quietly lower expectations, introduce factual errors, or expose student information. in this session teams first catch an error in a mock ai-leveled passage, then design supported versions of one science reading for fictional learner profiles and judge every design against four guardrails: same learning target, verified accuracy, protected privacy, and evidence of learning. ai3.1 ai3.3 ai4.1 ai2.1 i know how to leverage ai tools to personalize and enhance learning experiences. ai-enhanced learning i am aware of how ai can support diverse learning needs and styles. ai-enhanced learning i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship the monarch waystation: original and mock ai-leveled version learner profile cards guardrails rubric easier, or different? verify the leveled text supports that keep the target design for two learners guardrail gallery your real text 3-5 6-8 9-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"phish-tank-irl","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5","6-8"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":40,"areas":["S2.0","AI2.0","T2.0"],"text":"phish tank irl teams sort fourteen texts, emails, and game-chat messages into safe, scam, or check-another-way, and learn why ai-polished scams make spelling mistakes a useless clue. many of us were taught that scams are easy to spot because they're full of typos. now anyone can use ai to write a polished, friendly, perfectly spelled scam in seconds. in this gallery-walk sort, teams visit a \"tank\" of fourteen realistic messages, decide which are safe, which are scams, and which need to be checked another way, and name the red flags that still work. they leave with a slow down routine they can use on any message. s2.1 s2.3 ai2.2 t2.2 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i know how to guide students in safe and responsible internet use. digital literacy digital citizenship generative ai literacy message tank slow down check scam reply lab spot the scam in two seconds the gallery walk which flags still work? the slow down check scam reply lab one flag i'll remember 3-5 6-8 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"photo-detective","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":45,"areas":["S2.0","AI4.0","AI1.0","S5.0"],"text":"photo detective students investigate described photos that were cropped, edited, or made with ai, and learn two fact-checker moves that work better than staring: stop, and find better coverage. a photo can be real and still tell a false story. it can be **cropped** so you only see part of what happened, **edited** so something is added, removed, or changed, or **made with ai** so it shows something that never happened at all. staring harder at a picture often doesn't help, and ai-made images keep getting harder to spot by looking. in this lesson, teams investigate ten photo cards (described in words, so no real photos are needed). they practice two moves fact-checkers use, in kid language: **stop** (notice your feelings before you believe or share) and **find better coverage** (do other trusted sources show the same thing?). then they crop a picture themselves to see how easily a story can change. s2.2 ai4.1 ai1.3 s2.3 s5.1 i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver media literacy generative ai literacy photo cards photo detective log crop it! what's outside the frame? four ways a picture can fool you, two moves that help detective rounds which guesses changed? crop it! before i share 3-5 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"student-voice-policy-makers","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":180,"areas":["AI2.0","S3.0","S5.0"],"text":"policy makers student teams research, draft, and pitch an ai and technology-use policy proposal to a mock board, and must pass the same five-question check real leaders use. rules about ai and technology use are often written for students without students in the room. in this three-session project, students become policy makers. they analyze an outdated fictional acceptable-use policy, gather perspectives from classmates and staff, and draft a focused proposal on one issue (ai in coursework, phones, data privacy, or accessibility). every proposal must pass the five-question check that school leaders use: *what is the vision? what evidence will guide us? what changes in instruction? who might be left out? how will families understand the change?* teams then present to a mock board that asks hard questions. the strongest proposals can go to the real one. ai2.1 s3.2 s5.1 s3.3 ai2.2 i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver i am aware of the value of diverse perspectives in group work. collaborative learner i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship harmon creek high school technology policy (2019 excerpt) and stakeholder voices policy proposal builder mock board scoring rubric session 1: read the old rules session 1: map the issue and the stakeholders session 1: compare with the real policy session 2: draft the proposal session 2: the five-question check and red team session 3: rehearse session 3: the mock board hearing session 3: board feedback and next steps 9-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"presentation-makeover","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["medialit","digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":55,"areas":["S3.0","S1.0","AI2.0"],"text":"presentation makeover teams rescue a cluttered slide deck, making it clear, accessible, honestly sourced, and upfront about ai images, then pitch the revised version in 60 seconds. most students have sat through a slide deck with tiny yellow text, a paragraph per slide, a mystery statistic, and a photo pulled from somewhere. in this activity, teams get a printed \"before\" deck made by fictional students and give it a full makeover: one idea per slide, readable fonts and contrast, alt text for images, credit for every image and fact, and a clear note on any ai-generated image. then each team presents the revised version in 60 seconds. the point is that a presentation is communication, and honest, accessible design is part of communicating well. s3.2 s1.1 ai2.1 s1.3 i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i know how to use technology to support my learning. tech savvy i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of how ai can assist me in my studies. tech savvy media literacy digital citizenship generative ai literacy the \"before\" deck makeover checklist 60-second pitch rubric five seconds per slide audit the \"before\" deck think aloud: fixing slide 3 make it over 60-second pitches what changed and why 6-8 9-12 deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"primary-source-vs-ai-summary","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["AI4.0","S2.0","AI1.0","S3.0","T5.0"],"text":"primary source vs. ai summary students read a clearly fictional 1911 letter, then catch an ai summary that changes dates, invents a quote, turns a rumor into a fact, and erases the neighbors who helped. ai summaries are fast and fluent, which makes them easy to trust. but a summary is only useful if it is faithful to the source. students read a primary-source-style letter (fictional and written for this lesson) from a 13-year-old describing a flood in her town, then compare it line by line with a mock ai summary. the summary sounds right but contains six distortions: a wrong date, an exaggeration, an invented quote, an uncertain rumor stated as fact, an added detail, and a family whose help disappears. students learn a habit historians already use: go back to the source. ai4.1 s2.2 ai1.3 s3.2 t5.1 i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i know how to design lessons that promote higher-order thinking skills. critical thinking media literacy generative ai literacy the letter the ai summary distortion detector the telephone game, historian edition read the letter first distortion detectives why did the summary do that? write a faithful summary my summary rule 6-8 9-12 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting","type":"activity"},{"id":"private-or-share","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":25,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0","AI2.0"],"text":"private or share? children walk to a red, yellow, or green \"traffic light\" to sort personal information, then practice what to say when a game or talking computer asks for it. young children are asked for information all the time: by games, by video apps, by talking speakers, and sometimes by friendly-sounding chatbots. in this lesson the classroom becomes a traffic light. children carry information strips to **red (keep it private)**, **yellow (ask a grown-up first)**, or **green (ok to share)**, explain their choice, and then role-play what to do when \"chatty,\" a pretend game robot, asks for their address. they leave with a shield of private things and one sentence they can say out loud. s2.1 s2.3 s4.1 ai2.1 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use digital citizenship generative ai literacy traffic light sort chatty says… my private shield the treasure box the traffic light walk the traffic light chatty says… my private shield k-2 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"dashboard-critical-read","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","coaches","faculty"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["medialit","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["L3.0","AI1.0"],"text":"read the dashboard before it reads you leaders take apart eight mock dashboard panels, including an ai-generated \"insight,\" to practice asking what the data shows, what it can't show, and who could be harmed by a hasty conclusion. dashboards arrive with confidence: colors, arrows, and increasingly, automatically generated \"insights\" written in plain english. leaders are expected to act on them. in this session, teams read eight fictional dashboard panels with the same critical eye they'd teach students to bring to an infographic: checking axes, baselines, sample sizes, what the metric actually measures, and what the data can't tell you. they also examine what personal data each panel exposes and to whom. teams finish by redrawing one misleading panel honestly and writing a three-sentence summary a staff meeting could trust. l3.2 l3.1 l3.3 ai1.3 i can interpret complex digital data sets and communicate findings effectively using data visualization tools. data-informed decision making with digital tools i know how to use digital data tools to drive school improvement efforts. data-informed decision making with digital tools i am aware of various digital assessment tools and their appropriate uses in education. data-informed decision making with digital tools i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy media literacy digital citizenship dashboard panel cards can / can't tell organizer dashboard data use and privacy checklist the alarming chart panel stations name the moves redraw and rewrite privacy and use check next data conversation k-12 higher ed misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"real-fake-or-generated","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":45,"areas":["AI1.0","S2.0","AI4.0","T2.0"],"text":"real, fake, or generated? students hunt for the classic clues of ai-made images and cloned voices, discover how often those clues mislead, and learn to check the source instead. students have heard the tips: look for extra fingers, garbled text, too-smooth skin, a flat robotic voice. in this activity they judge eight media cards using clues alone, then check where each item came from. some \"obvious fakes\" turn out to be real, and some flawless images turn out to be generated. the big idea: clues are hints that expire as tools improve, but checking the source works on every kind of media, including things no one has invented yet. ai1.3 s2.2 ai4.1 s2.3 t2.3 i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i am aware of strategies to help students identify and combat misinformation. digital literacy media literacy generative ai literacy media cards clue vs. source log my before-i-share plan which clue would you trust? round 1: clues only think aloud: check the source round 2: source slips when clues lied my before-i-share plan 6-8 9-12 deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"real-pretend-or-changed","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":30,"areas":["S2.0","AI4.0","S3.0","AI1.0"],"text":"real, pretend, or changed? young detectives move, wiggle, or freeze to decide whether a picture or story is real, pretend on purpose, or changed to look real, and learn to ask a grown-up when they can't tell. pretend is wonderful. stories, cartoons, and drawings are made up on purpose, and everyone knows it. the tricky kind is a picture that has been **changed**, or made by a computer, so it *looks* real. in this game, you describe pictures and stories aloud, and children stand tall for **real**, wiggle for **pretend**, or freeze like a statue for **changed**. they practice three detective questions and learn the most important move of all: when you can't tell by looking, ask a grown-up and check. s2.2 ai4.1 s3.2 ai1.3 i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy media literacy generative ai literacy real, pretend, or changed? clue detective checklist my detective page \"i saw a dog as tall as a house!\" three kinds, three poses, three questions detective moves how does a picture get changed? my detective page k-2 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"real-remixed-or-made-up","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["3-5","6-8","9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["T2.0","AI4.0","S2.0"],"text":"real, remixed, or made up? a gallery walk through ten mock viral posts (deepfakes, cheapfakes, out-of-context photos, and a few real ones) that teaches teachers to check where media came from instead of squinting for glitches. synthetic media gets the headlines, but much misleading media is cheaper: a slowed-down clip, a cropped sign, a real photo with a false caption. and as generators improve, hunting for extra fingers and warped text becomes less reliable. in this gallery walk, pairs visit ten stations of mock media, label each one, and discover that the verification moves that work (who posted it, where's the original, what do others say) are the same whether the media was made by ai or by a video editor. then they plan how to teach those moves, including the honest answer \"can't tell yet.\" t2.3 t2.1 ai4.2 s2.2 i am aware of strategies to help students identify and combat misinformation. digital literacy i can teach students to critically evaluate online information. digital literacy i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety media literacy generative ai literacy gallery station cards gallery walk field notes bring it to students too strange to be real? five labels, three questions the gallery walk reveal the keys bring it to students 3-5 6-8 9-12 higher ed deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"remix-rights","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["S1.0","AI2.0","S2.0"],"text":"remix rights students sort real-world remix decisions using copyright, creative commons, and fair use, then write credit lines that include honest notes about ai help. \"it was on the internet, so it's free\" is one of the most common student beliefs, and it's wrong. almost everything people create is protected by copyright automatically, but creators can choose to share through creative commons licenses, and fair use allows some limited uses for things like commentary, criticism, and teaching. in this activity, students sort fourteen remix decisions, learn what the creative commons license symbols mean, and write credit lines for a project, including an honest note when ai helped. the goal is not fear of using anything; it's knowing how to remix responsibly. s1.3 ai2.1 ai2.3 s1.1 s2.3 i am aware of how ai can assist me in my studies. tech savvy i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i know how to use technology to support my learning. tech savvy i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety digital citizenship generative ai literacy remix decisions sort license cards credit line builder free because it's online? four big ideas license match-up remix decisions credit it right my remix rule 6-8 9-12 copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit","type":"activity"},{"id":"ripple-out-futures-wheel","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians","coaches"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":60,"areas":["T1.0","AI2.0","T3.0","AI5.0"],"text":"ripple out: a futures wheel for learning tables take one plausible future headline about ai in school, map its ripples out three rings deep, and then work backward to what they should start doing this semester. arguments about whether a technology will arrive rarely change what anyone does on monday. this session skips that argument. each table gets a fictional, near-future headline grounded in something that already exists, and uses the futures wheel (a mapping tool jerome glenn introduced in 1971) to trace first-, second-, and third-order consequences. tables color-code who benefits and who gets left out, then use backcasting, a planning method that starts from a preferred future and works backward, to name what they should be doing in classrooms and libraries this semester. t1.3 ai2.2 t3.3 ai5.2 i am aware of emerging technologies and their potential applications in education. technology integration i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i am aware of cultural differences and how they impact learning. inclusive teaching i can use ai as a tool for innovation in educational projects and research. ai collaboration and innovation generative ai literacy digital citizenship future headline cards futures wheel template backcasting planner already here one wheel, out loud build the wheel color it and check the signal gallery walk: find the orange backcast to this semester one line to keep k-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"robot-guesses","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":30,"areas":["AI1.0","S1.0","S5.0"],"text":"robot guesses children train a pretend robot with picture-word cards and discover that ai makes guesses from patterns, and that a guess can be wrong. young children already hear talking speakers and see apps that \"know\" things. in this game, the class becomes the teachers of **robo**, a pretend robot that can only learn from the example cards it is shown and can only notice color. robo makes confident guesses, gets some hilariously wrong, and gets better when the class gives it more kinds of examples. children leave with a simple, true idea they can say out loud: \"ai looks for patterns and makes guesses. guesses can be wrong, so we check.\" ai1.1 ai1.3 s1.3 s5.3 i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i am aware of how ai can assist me in my studies. tech savvy i am aware of how to apply computational thinking in various subjects. creative problem solver generative ai literacy pattern cards robo's training cards my robot guess clap, stomp, clap… pattern cards meet robo and train it test robo fix robo, then connect to real ai my robot guess k-2 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"screen-time-green-time","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":30,"areas":["S4.0","S3.0"],"text":"screen time, green time children sort everyday activities, notice how their bodies and feelings respond to sitting still and to moving, and make a family balance plan, learning that screens aren't bad and choices matter. children hear a lot of grown-up worry about screens. this lesson gives them something more useful than worry: noticing and choosing. children sort activity cards into **screen time**, **green time** (no screen), and **both**, and discover that screens can help us learn, create, and connect with people we love. then they do a short **body check-in**: sit still staring at a paper \"screen,\" then move, and notice what changes in their eyes, bodies, and feelings. they finish by drawing a balance plan they take home to make with their family. the message is honest: screens aren't bad or good by themselves; how we use them and what else we make time for is what matters. s4.3 s4.1 s4.2 s3.2 i am aware of my learning strengths and areas for improvement. self-directed learner i know how to take responsibility for my own learning. self-directed learner i can set achievable goals and monitor my progress. self-directed learner i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner digital citizenship screen or green? my body check-in our family balance plan thumbs up, thumbs down? screen or green? body check-in stop signals my balance plan one word k-2 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking","type":"activity"},{"id":"screens-at-school-screens-at-home","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians","leaders","staff"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":40,"areas":["L5.0","L4.0","T2.0"],"text":"screens at school, screens at home: a family conversation families sort everyday screen moments by purpose, learn in plain language what the texas phone law does and doesn't cover, and start a family media agreement that fits their own home. families hear two messages about screens: \"phones are banned at school now\" and \"kids use screens all day at school.\" both are partly true, and the difference matters. in this ready-to-run session, facilitators sit *with* families as they sort everyday screen moments from school and home by purpose, hear what texas hb 1481 (2025) does and doesn't cover, learn how the american academy of pediatrics' 2026 policy statement shifts the advice toward quality, context, and conversation, and start a family media agreement to finish at home. it stands alone or runs as one station inside a family night planned with the designing a family ai & digital citizenship night activity. there is no \"right\" family here: the goal is conversation, not judgment. l5.1 l5.3 l4.1 t2.2 i know how to build strong digital partnerships with families and community organizations. technology-enabled community engagement i am aware of strategies to leverage online community resources to support student learning. technology-enabled community engagement i know how to create a positive and inclusive digital learning climate. fostering a digital learning culture i know how to guide students in safe and responsible internet use. digital literacy digital citizenship media literacy screen-moment cards what the law says, what doctors say, what we decide our family media agreement starter a screen moment you liked sort the screen moments what the law says, and doesn't what doctors say start a family media agreement one thing to try, one question for us k-12 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"seminar-on-a-machines-answer","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["3-5","6-8","9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["T5.0","AI4.0"],"text":"seminar on a machine's answer a fishbowl socratic seminar where the \"text\" is a chatbot's confident, partly wrong answer, and the chatbot's second answer is worse, so teachers practice the questions that make students think instead of accept. a socratic seminar usually centers on a rich text: a poem, a primary source, a court opinion. what happens when the text is an ai chatbot's answer? this session uses a mock transcript in which a chatbot explains why earth has seasons. it gets the main idea right, slips in a common misconception, and then, when the student pushes back with a wrong idea, apologizes and agrees. teachers run the seminar as students, track which questions deepen the thinking, and then design a seminar around an ai output in their own subject. t5.2 ai4.1 ai4.2 t5.1 i can use questioning techniques to stimulate deep learning. critical thinking i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking i know how to design lessons that promote higher-order thinking skills. critical thinking generative ai literacy media literacy the seasons transcript question stem cards design your own ai-text seminar would you accept this answer? read and annotate the fishbowl as teachers: which questions did the work? design your own ai-text seminar 3-5 6-8 9-12 higher ed copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting","type":"activity"},{"id":"signal-to-headline-lab","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["S2.0","AI4.0","S5.0","AI1.0"],"text":"signal to headline lab students turn a true present-day tech trend into a plausible future headline and lede, then trade and fact-check each other's work for accuracy and clickbait. futures researchers watch for **signals**: small, real pieces of evidence that something is changing. in this lab, student pairs draw a signal card describing something true about technology today, decide where it could plausibly lead, and write the news story from that future as a journalist would: a headline, a lede, and an evidence line. then pairs trade and fact-check each other's work. does the headline match the story? does it trace back to something real, or is it just possible? is it news or clickbait? students finish by proposing one rule or habit their generation should adopt now. s2.2 ai4.1 s5.2 ai1.3 s2.3 i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i can use creativity and logic to find innovative solutions. creative problem solver i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety media literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship signal cards future news draft headline fact-check which would you click? which would you trust? signal, cone, headline, lede write the news from the future trade and fact-check one rule for our generation exit ticket 6-8 9-12 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"smart-speakers-smart-choices","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2","3-5"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":35,"areas":["S2.0","AI1.0"],"text":"smart speakers, smart choices children role-play with a pretend talking speaker to learn that voice helpers listen for a wake word, may keep what they hear, answer by guessing, and sometimes need a grown-up instead. many children talk to smart speakers, talking toys, and voice assistants on phones or tablets at home, and they often treat them like a friend who knows everything. in this role-play, a classmate wearing a paper \"speaker\" becomes **pip**, a pretend voice helper. the class discovers three true things: pip waits for its wake word and then listens closely, pip may save what it hears on a far-away computer, and pip answers by guessing from patterns, so it can be wrong. children practice sorting what is ok to say to a device, what to keep private, and when to ask a grown-up instead. s2.1 s2.3 ai1.1 ai1.3 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy digital citizenship generative ai literacy pip's script cards talk-to-pip sort my smart speaker promise who is listening? meet pip: three true things talk-to-pip sort student pips what did pip teach us? my smart speaker promise k-2 3-5 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"spreadsheet-sense-making","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["S5.0","S1.0","AI4.0"],"text":"spreadsheet sense-making students turn a lunch survey into a spreadsheet or paper grid, compute counts and averages, build an honest chart, then catch and fix charts (and an ai chart suggestion) that mislead with a truncated axis or a cherry-picked range. charts show up everywhere students look: school newsletters, ads, news stories, social posts, and more and more often, ai tools that offer to \"make a chart for you.\" in this lesson, students become the data team for the fictional cedar ridge middle school cafeteria. they enter 24 survey responses into a spreadsheet (or a paper grid), count favorites and average the menu ratings, and draw an honest chart. then they meet two flashy charts that use the same kind of data to tell a misleading story, one with a **truncated axis** and one with a **cherry-picked range**, plus a confident ai chart suggestion that gets the math wrong. students fix each one and write a rule they can use whenever a chart tries to persuade them. s5.1 s1.1 s5.2 ai4.1 i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver i know how to use technology to support my learning. tech savvy i can use creativity and logic to find innovative solutions. creative problem solver i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking media literacy generative ai literacy cedar ridge lunch survey data charts that tell a story, and a chatbot's suggestion sense-making worksheet pizza wins by a landslide? build the data grid make an honest chart catch the chart tricks check the chatbot my chart-checking rule 6-8 9-12 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"student-voice-data-dive","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","leaders"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["C3.0","C5.0","T4.0","AI4.0"],"text":"student voice data dive coaches and teachers design a short, privacy-safe student survey on devices, ai, and wellbeing, then read returned results with notice-wonder-next and act on what students said. campuses make decisions about screens, ai, and phone rules every week, usually without asking the students who live with them. this session treats student voice as instructional data. teams design a five-question anonymous survey and a short interview on when technology helps or hinders learning and wellbeing, check it against a privacy checklist, and then read a fictional set of returned results from a seventh-grade team using notice-wonder-next. the protocol keeps the team describing before interpreting and reframing problems as design choices the team controls, and it ends with one change and a plan to tell students what their answers changed. c3.1 c3.2 c5.2 t4.3 ai4.1 i know how to use data to inform instructional decisions. data-driven coaching i can help teachers interpret and act on assessment results. data-driven coaching i can facilitate productive discussions and problem-solving sessions. collaborative leadership i am aware of the importance of emotional support in academic success. relationship building i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking digital citizenship generative ai literacy privacy-safe student voice checklist returned results, mesquite ridge grade 7 notice-wonder-next organizer predict before you look design a five-question survey privacy swap read the returned results with notice-wonder-next one change and a \"you said, we did\" what did students see that we didn't? k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"team-tech-choices","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":45,"areas":["S1.0","S3.0","S4.0"],"text":"team tech choices teams plan a real group project by matching each job to the right tool, or no tool at all, defending their choices, and setting goals they can track together. \"we made a slideshow\" isn't evidence of learning. choosing *why* a tool fits the job is. in this lesson teams plan a **school garden guide for first graders**. they draw task cards (find out what first graders want to know, count visiting birds, write in kid-friendly words, share with families) and tool cards (paper and pencil, a library book, a tally chart, a class tablet camera, a teacher-driven ai chatbot, talking to an expert, or no tool at all), and match them. each match must answer three questions: what's the job? which tool does it best? what will it show we learned? teams defend choices in a gallery challenge, learn how they'd handle a brand-new tool, and set goals with checkpoints. s1.1 s3.1 s1.2 s3.3 s4.2 i know how to use technology to support my learning. tech savvy i know how to work effectively in a team. collaborative learner i can adapt to new digital tools and platforms. tech savvy i am aware of the value of diverse perspectives in group work. collaborative learner i can set achievable goals and monitor my progress. self-directed learner digital citizenship generative ai literacy task and tool cards tool choice planner team goal and roles spoon, hammer, or banana? roles and the three questions match the jobs to the tools gallery challenge and the mystery tool set the team goal why this tool? 3-5 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"ai-critic-tank","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["6-8","9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"digital","minutes":90,"areas":["T5.0","AI2.0","AI5.0"],"text":"the ai critic tank teams design a solution to a local community problem by hand, invite an ai chatbot to tear it apart (as critic, never author), decide which critiques to accept, disclose the ai's role, and pitch to a panel. when students use ai on a project, it too often writes the idea and the student decorates it. this session flips the roles. teams of teachers, working as students, choose a problem in the fictional town of cedar bend, understand it, and design a solution entirely on their own. only then do they ask an approved chatbot to act as a skeptical critic. they log every critique, accept, reject, or modify each one with a reason, revise, write an honest disclosure of how ai was used, and pitch to a \"tank\" panel. the debrief turns the structure into a problem-solving project teachers can run with students. t5.3 t5.1 ai2.1 ai5.1 ai2.3 i am aware of strategies to develop students' problem-solving abilities. critical thinking i know how to design lessons that promote higher-order thinking skills. critical thinking i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i know how to foster collaboration between humans and ai systems. ai collaboration and innovation i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship cedar bend problem cards ai critique log tank panel rubric author or critic? understand the problem design it by hand call in the critic revise and disclose the tank as teachers: where was the thinking? plan the student version 6-8 9-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"the-coaching-agreement","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","leaders"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":55,"areas":["C3.0","C5.0","C1.0","L2.0"],"text":"the coaching agreement a coach and principal analyze a coaching-time audit together, including the drift that ai and tech rollouts cause, and write a one-page agreement before the coaching cycle begins. by november, many coaches find their weeks filled with testing coordination, class coverage, and resetting passwords for the latest platform, while coaching cycles quietly stall. this session comes before the tcea eiir coaching cycle begins. a coach and a principal read a fictional two-week coaching-time audit, sort where the time went, and name the sources of role drift, including a campus ai rollout. then they walk through a six-step conversation and write a one-page agreement: the role, the evaluation boundary, what the coach won't take on, a realistic caseload, how coaching logs stay private, and what ai may and may not do with them. c3.1 c5.1 c1.1 l2.1 i know how to use data to inform instructional decisions. data-driven coaching i know how to foster a collaborative culture among educators. collaborative leadership i know how to mentor teachers in effective instructional practices. teacher support i know how to support and evaluate effective technology integration practices. technology-enhanced instructional leadership digital citizenship generative ai literacy coaching-time audit, sycamore springs elementary six-step agreement conversation coach–principal agreement guess your own number read the audit model the hardest step: the evaluation boundary the six-step conversation write the one-page agreement what will we measure in november? k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works","type":"activity"},{"id":"the-feed-game","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["S2.0","AI1.0","S5.0"],"text":"the feed game students play a card-based recommendation engine by hand and watch their feed shrink to a few topics, then design ways to take it back. recommendation systems don't know what is true, kind, or good for you. they predict what will keep you watching, based on patterns in what you did before. in this game, one student plays the algorithm and follows a printed rule card to choose posts for a viewer, scoring every reaction. in five rounds, most feeds narrow to one or two topics, and posts designed to spark strong feelings rise to the top. students then change one rule and discover what they can and can't control. s2.3 ai1.1 s5.3 s2.2 i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i am aware of how to apply computational thinking in various subjects. creative problem solver i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety media literacy generative ai literacy feed cards feed tracker take back your feed why did that show up? how to play five rounds what the algorithm knew change one rule take back your feed 6-8 9-12 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"five-question-rollout-check","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","coaches"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":60,"areas":["L1.0","L2.0","AI2.0"],"text":"the five-question rollout check leadership teams role-play the planning meeting for a campus-wide platform rollout and discover that the questions that matter most come after \"should we buy it?\" a principal wants every department on a new learning platform with a built-in ai writing-feedback feature, starting in six weeks. in this tabletop, each participant takes a stakeholder role, including an eighth grader who reports that the feedback panel does not work with her screen reader, and the table must reach a rollout decision while timed injects keep arriving. the debrief turns the conversation into the five-question check from the updated eles: what is the vision? what evidence will guide us? what changes in instruction? who might be left out? how will families understand the change? technology purchases are easy to announce. rollouts that improve learning start with those five questions. l1.1 l1.2 l2.2 ai2.2 i know how to create and communicate a compelling vision for technology integration in the school. visionary technology leadership i can develop strategic technology plans that align with educational trends and community needs. visionary technology leadership i can guide the implementation of digital curriculum and instructional technology initiatives. technology-enhanced instructional leadership i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use digital citizenship generative ai literacy the rollout memo role and inject cards five-question check organizer the memo everyone has received step into a role the planning meeting, with injects run the five-question check compare decisions across tables your real initiative k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"glass-box-checklist","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","librarians","coaches","teachers"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["AI2.0","AI1.0"],"text":"the glass box checklist teams interrogate a fictional ai vendor's glossy fact sheet, sort the questions that actually matter, and build a transparency and accountability checklist they can use on real tools. ai tools arrive in schools with confident marketing: \"safe,\" \"unbiased,\" \"research-based,\" \"personalized.\" transparency means being able to see behind those words: what the tool does, what data it uses, where it fails, and who answers for it when it's wrong. in this session teams read a fictional vendor fact sheet, sort a bank of evaluation questions, and build a short checklist that turns vague reassurance into answerable questions. then they test the checklist on the published documentation of a tool their district already uses or is considering. ai2.3 ai2.1 ai1.3 i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship mock vendor fact sheet: brightpath writing coach question sort glass box starter checklist glass box or black box? read the fact sheet sort the questions build your checklist test it on a real tool what's still unanswered? k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"human-language-model","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians","coaches","faculty"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":50,"areas":["AI1.0","AI4.0"],"text":"the human language model educators become a tiny language model with paper slips and a cup, and discover firsthand why fluent ai text can be confidently wrong. generative ai chatbots don't look facts up the way a search engine or a database does. they generate text by repeatedly predicting a likely next piece of text based on patterns learned from enormous amounts of training data. in this unplugged session, teams \"train\" a ten-sentence language model by hand, generate new sentences by drawing slips from a cup, and then ask their model a factual question. the fluent, plausible, wrong answers that come out are the most memorable explanation of hallucination most educators will ever get, and one they can run with students the next day. ai1.1 ai1.2 ai1.3 ai4.1 i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i can explain ai concepts in simple terms to various stakeholders. ai literacy i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy media literacy the tiny training library next-word tally sheet model run cards finish my sentence train your model generate: draw the next word ask your model a fact honest differences: paper vs. real models your one-minute explanation where does ai belong? k-12 higher ed copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"the-prompt-ladder","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["T1.0","AI1.0","AI3.0","AI2.0"],"text":"the prompt ladder teachers climb eight rungs of prompts for the same classroom task, predict each mock ai output before reading it, and weigh what every added detail gains and what it costs. a chatbot predicts its answer from patterns it learned plus the words you give it, so your prompt is the context it predicts from. adding detail usually narrows the output toward what you want, but not always for free. in this unplugged session, teams climb a \"ladder\" of eight prompts for one real teacher task (a 4th-grade water-cycle exit ticket). at each rung, they predict what the ai will produce, read a printed mock output, and score the tradeoff. two rungs are traps: one where a prompt's built-in misconception gets repeated back as fact, and one that works well but pastes private student information into the tool. teachers leave with a prompting habit they can model for students: add the context that serves the learning, and stop before it costs accuracy or privacy. t1.2 ai1.1 t1.3 ai3.1 ai2.1 i can use ai tools to enhance student learning experiences. technology integration i know how ai systems work and their capabilities in educational contexts. ai literacy i am aware of emerging technologies and their potential applications in education. technology integration i know how to leverage ai tools to personalize and enhance learning experiences. ai-enhanced learning i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship prompt rung cards mock outputs prompt tradeoff scorecard more words, narrower guesses play the chatbot climb the ladder the two traps, out loud where would you stop climbing? your own three rungs k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting","type":"activity"},{"id":"shallow-answers-coaching-cycle","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":60,"areas":["C1.0","C3.0","AI4.0"],"text":"the shallow answers coaching cycle coaches role-play a full listen, co-plan, model, measure, adjust cycle on one familiar problem: students finish the digital work but say very little. a teacher tells you students are completing every digital activity but their answers are shallow, and some read as if a chatbot wrote them. this simulation walks coaches through a whole coaching cycle on that one case: listening before fixing, co-planning a lesson with fewer questions and more reasoning, modeling a move, and measuring the change in real student work. the cycle ends where the 2026 eles say coaching should: \"what changed in teacher practice or student evidence?\" c1.1 c1.2 c3.2 ai4.1 i know how to mentor teachers in effective instructional practices. teacher support i can provide constructive feedback to improve teaching strategies. teacher support i can help teachers interpret and act on assessment results. data-driven coaching i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy media literacy role-play cards student evidence samples: week 1 and week 3 coaching cycle planner same question, two answers listen first (round 1 role-play) think aloud: co-planning fewer, better questions co-plan and model (round 2 role-play) measure and adjust what changed in teacher practice or student evidence? pick your real teacher k-12 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"tool-tryout-lab","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":90,"areas":["C2.0","C5.0","C4.0","AI3.0"],"text":"tool tryout lab instead of a demo and a vote, teacher teams test an emerging ai feature inside a grits lesson-study loop, watch students rather than the tool, and decide from student evidence. teachers ask \"can we use this?\" about a new ai feature almost weekly. the usual answer is a demo and a show of hands. this lab replaces the vote with a structured tryout built on the grits lesson study model: the team sets a learning goal from student data, picks the research-based strategy the tool must serve, tries the fictional marginmate annotation tool's new ai hints feature as learners, plans a classroom tryout where colleagues observe students instead of the teacher, and then analyzes returned student work from two teams to reach a decision with a rubric. the possible decisions are adopt, adjust and retry, or drop, and adjust is the most common honest answer. c2.2 c2.3 c5.2 c4.1 ai3.2 i can evaluate the effectiveness of educational technology tools. technology integration i am aware of emerging technologies and their potential in education. technology integration i can facilitate productive discussions and problem-solving sessions. collaborative leadership i know how to design and deliver effective professional development sessions. professional development i can evaluate the effectiveness of ai-enhanced learning interventions. ai-enhanced learning generative ai literacy media literacy digital citizenship marginmate tryout packet tryout decision rubric grits tryout plan \"can we use this?\" g and r: goal first, then strategy what observers watch i: plan the tryout t: two weeks later, decide from the evidence s: strengthen and share your next tryout k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"two-drafts-of-feedback","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","leaders"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":50,"areas":["C1.0","AI2.0"],"text":"two drafts of feedback coaches score a human-written and an ai-drafted feedback note on the same lesson transcript, then set the ground rules for when ai belongs in observation, if it belongs at all. generative ai can turn a lesson transcript into a tidy feedback note in seconds, and some coaching platforms now offer to do it automatically. coaches in this session read a short fictional transcript, score two unlabeled feedback drafts against a quality checklist, and guess which one a chatbot wrote. the reveal sets up the harder conversation: consent, student privacy, transparency with the teacher, and the line between an ai draft and a coach's judgment. c1.2 ai2.3 c1.1 ai2.1 i can provide constructive feedback to improve teaching strategies. teacher support i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i know how to mentor teachers in effective instructional practices. teacher support i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use generative ai literacy digital citizenship lesson transcript and two feedback drafts feedback quality checklist ai in observation sort which one did a person write? read the lesson, then score both drafts the reveal and the trace sort the uses: consent, privacy, transparency write the feedback mr. delgado deserves our disclosure sentence k-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"vendor-claims-vs-evidence","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","faculty","coaches"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["medialit","genai","digcit"],"mode":"digital","minutes":75,"areas":["L1.0","AI3.0","L3.0","AI2.0"],"text":"two grade levels? vendor claims vs. evidence leaders trace an ai product's headline result back to the study behind it, read the terms of service line by line, and design the local pilot that would actually answer \"does it work here?\" \"students improved two grade levels!\" is the kind of claim that moves budgets. it is also a claim leaders are rarely taught to trace. in this session, teams read a fictional ai tutoring product's marketing, the efficacy summary behind the headline, and excerpts from its terms of service. they sort statements into strong evidence, weak evidence, and privacy flags, then apply the same moves online to a real product their district is considering. the session ends with a design for a small local pilot, because the question that matters is not whether a product worked somewhere, but whether it improves learning for your students. l1.3 ai3.2 l3.3 ai2.3 i am aware of emerging educational technologies and their potential impact on teaching and learning. visionary technology leadership i can evaluate the effectiveness of ai-enhanced learning interventions. ai-enhanced learning i am aware of various digital assessment tools and their appropriate uses in education. data-informed decision making with digital tools i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use media literacy generative ai literacy digital citizenship the stepup ai tutor packet evidence or red flag? sort claim-to-evidence ledger the headline read the packet with three highlighters evidence or red flag? now a real product design the local pilot back to the headline k-12 higher ed misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"verify-the-bot-workshop","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians","coaches"],"grades":["6-8","9-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"digital","minutes":60,"areas":["AI4.0"],"text":"verify the bot science teachers split confident ai explanations into checkable claims, verify each with two independent sources, and leave with a 15-minute workshop to teach the routine to colleagues. ai chatbots explain science fluently, and they can blend accurate explanations with classic misconceptions, invented citations, and numbers that sound right. the fix isn't \"don't trust ai\" or \"trust ai\"; it's a routine: split the answer into claims, check each claim against two independent sources, then keep, fix, or cut. participants practice the routine on printed mock ai answers about seasons and plant growth, run it live on a district-approved chatbot, and then plan a 15-minute version they can lead for their department, because teaching colleagues to verify is how the habit spreads to students. ai4.2 ai4.1 ai4.3 i can teach others to question and verify ai outputs. ai and critical thinking i know how to promote critical thinking skills in relation to ai-generated information. ai and critical thinking i am aware of strategies to develop ai-related problem-solving skills. ai and critical thinking generative ai literacy media literacy mock ai science answers claim ledger 15-minute workshop planner sounds right to me the routine: split, source, compare, decide ledger race live round: check a real answer plan your 15-minute workshop what will students turn in? 6-8 9-12 copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting","type":"activity"},{"id":"wait-time-on-the-record","audience":"pl","roles":["coaches","leaders"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["C1.0","C3.0","C2.0","AI2.0"],"text":"wait time on the record coaches code a consented, timestamped classroom clip for questioning and wait time, test an ai summary against the timestamps, and write feedback a teacher can act on tomorrow. video is one of the most useful things a coach can bring to a feedback conversation and one of the easiest to misuse. this session walks the micro-teaching video cycle (empower, implement, investigate, realize), which mirrors the implement and improve into reflect and realize phases of the tcea eiir coaching cycle. coaches read the consent agreement and goal a teacher set, code a fictional timestamped transcript of a fifth-grade discussion about whether a viral hailstone photo is real, and measure wait time by hand with a notice-wonder-next protocol. then they test a confident ai-generated summary against their own data, decide whether an ai transcription tool belongs in their coaching at all, and write glows-and-grows feedback anchored to what students did. c1.2 c3.3 c2.2 ai2.1 c1.1 i can provide constructive feedback to improve teaching strategies. teacher support i am aware of various data collection and analysis tools. data-driven coaching i can evaluate the effectiveness of educational technology tools. technology integration i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i know how to mentor teachers in effective instructional practices. teacher support generative ai literacy digital citizenship media literacy consent agreement, transcript, and ai summary question and wait time log consent, privacy, and feedback checklist five seconds of silence empower: consent and a goal before the camera investigate: code the transcript by hand test the ai summary against the timestamps realize: glows, grows, and a new target deliver it and listen your campus version k-12 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"what-do-my-apps-know","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"digital","minutes":60,"areas":["T2.0","S2.0","AI2.0"],"text":"what do my apps know? teachers read a mock app privacy policy the way a 12-year-old would, then audit the real tools in their own classroom and decide what to keep, change, or replace. every app, extension, and ai tool we put in front of students collects something: names, voices, writing, clicks, sometimes location. teachers are often the first, and last, person to decide whether a tool is used, yet few of us have read the policies. in this session, participants first experience a mock privacy policy for a fictional ai story app from a student's point of view, then use a 12-item audit to review two real tools they use, and leave with a plan to teach students the same habit of asking \"what does this app know about me?\" t2.2 s2.1 ai2.1 ai2.3 i know how to guide students in safe and responsible internet use. digital literacy i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use digital citizenship storybuddy ai privacy policy (mock) tool privacy audit my classroom tool inventory count the data trail read it like a 12-year-old think aloud with the audit audit your own tools what we found, what we'll change plan the student version k-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion","type":"activity"},{"id":"what-your-map-app-knows","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":45,"areas":["S2.0","S5.0"],"text":"what your map app knows students plot a fictional teen's saturday from location clues on a town map, discover how much a trail of pings reveals, weigh safety against privacy, and write a location settings checkup plan. phones, games, cameras, and friend-finder apps can record where we are, often without us thinking about it. in this unplugged simulation, teams receive twelve location clues from one saturday in the life of dani, a fictional seventh grader in the fictional town of juniper falls: map app pings, a geotagged photo, a check-in, a live location share. teams plot the trail on a printed town grid and discover that a handful of dots reveals where dani lives, who dani hangs out with, and where dani will probably be next saturday. then students weigh when sharing location keeps people safe and when it puts them at risk, and write a step-by-step checkup plan for their own device settings. s2.1 s2.3 s5.1 i know how to protect my personal information online. online safety i am aware of the potential risks of sharing content online. online safety i know how to approach complex problems systematically. creative problem solver digital citizenship juniper falls town grid dani's saturday location clues my location settings checkup plan three dots plot dani's saturday what the trail reveals four corners: safety vs. privacy my checkup plan exit ticket 6-8 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance","type":"activity"},{"id":"who-gets-left-out-map","audience":"pl","roles":["leaders","staff","faculty"],"grades":["K-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":75,"areas":["L4.0","L1.0","AI2.0"],"text":"who gets left out? mapping the digital divide leaders walk a proposed homework-and-ai-tutor initiative through the lives of ten fictional families, map every barrier they hit, and turn the map into policy. \"every student has a device\" is where most equity conversations end, and where the real digital divide begins. it runs through home connectivity, shared devices, accessibility, home languages, caregivers' schedules, trust, and now the cost of ai tools, where free and paid tiers can give different students very different help. in this session, teams take a fictional district's plan to move homework online and add an ai tutor, then walk it through ten family profiles. every barrier goes on a wall map, and the map becomes the draft of an equitable-access policy with evidence to watch. l4.2 l4.3 l1.2 ai2.2 i can develop policies that support equitable access to technology and digital resources. fostering a digital learning culture i am aware of strategies to address digital divides and promote responsible technology use. fostering a digital learning culture i can develop strategic technology plans that align with educational trends and community needs. visionary technology leadership i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use digital citizenship the initiative and the district family profile cards barrier-to-policy map the confident plan walk the plan through ten families read the wall from barriers to policy revise the plan your own map k-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"who-made-this","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":30,"areas":["S2.0","S1.0","AI2.0","S3.0"],"text":"who made this? children discover that every picture, video, song, and game was made by someone for a reason, and practice asking \"who made this, and why?\" young children often experience media as something that just *appears* on a screen or a shelf. in this lesson they meet the makers behind it. you describe familiar kinds of media (a picture book, a cereal box, a cartoon, a game, a school poster, a picture made with an ai tool), and children answer two detective questions: **who made this?** (a person, a company, or a person using ai) and **why did they make it?** (to teach, to sell, to have fun, or to tell news). children walk to \"why\" corners, then become makers themselves. the big idea they carry home: every piece of media was made by someone, on purpose, and good makers are honest about how they made it. s2.2 s1.3 ai2.3 s3.2 i can identify trustworthy sources of information on the internet. online safety i am aware of how ai can assist me in my studies. tech savvy i am aware of the importance of transparency and accountability in ai systems. ethical ai use i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner media literacy generative ai literacy maker cards i am a maker maker hunt at home who made my picture? two detective questions walk to the why made with ai i am a maker say it together k-2 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting","type":"activity"},{"id":"who-owns-the-output","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":60,"areas":["AI2.0","S3.0"],"text":"who owns the output? in a structured academic controversy, students argue both sides of an open question: is it fair for ai tools to learn from artists' work, and who gets credit for what they make? generative image, music, and writing tools learned their patterns from enormous collections of human-made work, often gathered without asking the creators. whether that is fair, whether it is legal, and who owns what the tools produce are questions that courts, lawmakers, artists, and companies are still actively disputing, and the answers differ from country to country. instead of a winner-take-all debate, students use a structured academic controversy: each pair argues one side, then switches and argues the other, then drops the sides and searches for the best shared recommendation. the goal is not a verdict on the law; it is the ability to reason carefully about an unsettled ethical question and act respectfully toward creators in the meantime. ai2.2 ai2.1 s3.3 s3.2 i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use i know how to apply ethical principles when using or developing ai for education. ethical ai use i am aware of the value of diverse perspectives in group work. collaborative learner i can communicate my ideas clearly to others. collaborative learner generative ai literacy digital citizenship the case of the cedar bluff poster perspective cards controversy organizer line up on it how training data works, and what's unsettled pairs prepare position a or b present, restate, switch drop the sides: build a recommendation line up again 9-12 higher ed copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards","type":"activity"},{"id":"whose-story-is-missing","audience":"pl","roles":["teachers","librarians"],"grades":["K-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit","digcit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":60,"areas":["T3.0","AI1.0","AI2.0"],"text":"whose story is missing? teachers audit eight mock ai-generated images and texts for who shows up, who doesn't, and who gets flattened, then fix one with human judgment and universal design for learning (udl). teachers increasingly use ai to generate classroom images, word problems, stories, and translations. those outputs can quietly repeat whatever was most common in the data a model learned from: one kind of family, one kind of scientist, one version of a town's history. in this session, teams audit eight mock ai outputs for representation, name the likely cause of each pattern, and decide whether a better prompt or a human fix is needed. then they revise one output so every student could see themselves in it and access it, using udl and basic accessibility checks. t3.3 t3.1 t3.2 ai1.3 ai2.2 i am aware of cultural differences and how they impact learning. inclusive teaching i know how to create a welcoming classroom environment for all students. inclusive teaching i can adapt my teaching methods to accommodate diverse learning needs. inclusive teaching i am aware of the current limitations and potential biases in ai systems. ai literacy i can identify potential ethical issues in ai applications and propose solutions. ethical ai use generative ai literacy media literacy digital citizenship mock ai output cards representation audit inclusive and accessible materials check picture a scientist audit the outputs why it happens, and why \"add diversity\" isn't enough fix one for real gallery and hard questions a student version k-12 deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community","type":"activity"},{"id":"op:k2-robot-or-person","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S1.0","AI1.0"],"text":"robot or person? i asked, \"what is your favorite food?\" one helper said, \"pizza, because my grandma makes it on fridays!\" the other said, \"many people like pizza.\" which one was a robot? the first one the second one not sure yet read both answers twice, then students hold up one finger for the first helper, two for the second, or a fist for not sure. ask: \"what clue helped you decide?\" the second one sounds like a robot. a robot does not eat or have a grandma; it guesses words that often go together. s1.3 ai1.1 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-apple-robot-guess","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S5.0","AI1.0"],"text":"what will robot guess? our pretend robot only ever saw red apples. now it sees a green apple. what will it guess, and why?  partners turn knee to knee and each share a guess and a reason. call on three pairs, then ask: \"how could we help the robot guess better next time?\" the robot might say \"not an apple\" because it learned that apples are red. robots guess from the patterns they have seen, so showing it more kinds of apples helps. s5.3 ai1.1 ai1.3 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy k-2 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works"},{"id":"op:k2-check-it-twice","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":7,"areas":["S4.0","AI4.0","AI1.0"],"text":"robot says: check it! a robot helper said, \"cats have wings and can fly to the moon!\" is that a fact or fake? fact fake students show thumbs up for fact or thumbs down for fake, then name two places they could check (a book, a grown-up, a trusted website). optional: you type a simple question into a district-approved chatbot on the projector and the class checks its answer in a book. fake. robot helpers can sound very sure and still be wrong, so we check in two places before we believe them. s4.1 ai4.1 ai1.3 fact or fake daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy media literacy k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-secret-or-share","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S2.0","AI2.0"],"text":"secret or share? a game asks you some questions. which ones are ok to share, and which ones stay secret? my favorite color my home address my pet's silly trick my password my full name and school read each item; students pat their head for \"ok to share\" or put a finger on their lips for \"keep it secret.\" ask: \"if a game or a talking computer asks for a secret, who do you tell?\" favorite color and a pet's trick are ok. address, password, full name, and school stay private, and you tell a trusted grown-up if anything asks for them. s2.1 ai2.1 sort daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship k-2 scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security"},{"id":"op:k2-kind-click-thumbs","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S3.0","S2.0"],"text":"kind click thumbs tomas sends a message: \"your drawing is so cool!\" then pip sends: \"your drawing is ugly.\" which one is a kind click? thumbs up: kind thumbs down: unkind read each message separately; students give thumbs up or down. then pairs turn pip's message into a kind one, and ask: \"what should you do if someone sends you an unkind message?\"  s3.2 s2.3 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship k-2 kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying"},{"id":"op:k2-rainbow-wing-dog","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","AI4.0"],"text":"rainbow wing dog picture a puppy with rainbow wings flying over a school. is it real, pretend on purpose, or changed to look real? real pretend on purpose changed to look real describe the picture aloud, or show a teacher-made drawing on the projector. students stand still for real, wiggle for pretend, or freeze like a statue for changed, and then you ask: \"what clue did you use, and who could you ask if you weren't sure?\" puppies do not have wings, so it is pretend or changed. a cartoon is pretend on purpose; a photo made to look real is changed, and a grown-up can help you tell. s2.2 ai4.1 image talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy generative ai literacy k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-who-made-it-why","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S3.0","S2.0"],"text":"who made it? why? a cartoon tiger on a cereal box says, \"eat zoomy o's every day!\" who made that tiger, and why?  partners take turns saying \"i think ___ made it because ___.\" share a few, then ask: \"is the tiger helping you or selling to you?\" the cereal company made the tiger to get people to buy zoomy o's. every picture and video was made by someone for a reason. s3.2 s2.2 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-move-or-sit","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0"],"text":"move or sit? i will say things kids like to do. stand up if it moves your body. sit down if you sit still. jumping rope watching a show playing tag a tablet game dancing reading a book call out each activity and let students stand or sit. then ask: \"how does your body feel after moving? after sitting a long time?\"  s4.3 s4.1 stand up, sit down daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship k-2 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time"},{"id":"op:k2-speaker-or-grown-up","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S1.0","AI1.0","S2.0"],"text":"speaker or grown-up? a talking speaker can help with some things. for which ones should you ask a grown-up instead? play a song set a timer i got hurt is this berry safe to eat? what is the weather? read each one; students point to the ceiling for \"speaker can help\" or to you for \"ask a grown-up.\" ask: \"why is a grown-up better for some questions?\" songs, timers, and weather are fine for a speaker. getting hurt or eating berries needs a grown-up, because the speaker only guesses and cannot see you. s1.1 ai1.1 s2.3 sort daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy digital citizenship k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-draw-a-helper-bot","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":10,"areas":["S5.0","AI2.0"],"text":"draw a helper bot pretend a robot helper comes to our class someday. draw one helpful thing it does, and one rule it must follow.  students draw on scrap paper or whiteboards for five minutes, then share with a neighbor. ask: \"why does your robot need that rule?\"  s5.2 ai2.2 draw daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy k-2 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:k2-one-big-drawing","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S3.0"],"text":"team drawing choice our class is making a picture of our school garden. would you rather each draw alone, or all add to one big drawing together? each draw alone one big drawing together students walk to one side of the room for each choice. each side shares one reason, then ask: \"how could we use both ideas?\"  s3.1 s3.3 would you rather daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-password-stand-up","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0"],"text":"password keepers a password is like a toothbrush: it's just for you. stand up if it's ok to share your password with this person. my best friend my grown-up at home a game character my teacher at school a kid in a video call out each one; students stand or stay seated. ask: \"why is it ok for your grown-up to know it, but not your best friend?\" only your grown-ups at home and your teacher (for school logins) should know it. friends, game characters, and people in videos do not get your password. s2.1 s4.1 stand up, sit down daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship k-2 scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security"},{"id":"op:k2-prize-pop-up","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0","S2.0"],"text":"you won a prize? you are playing a game and a box pops up: \"you won a prize! tap here!\" what do you do?  partners act it out: one reads the pop-up, the other shows what they would do. ask: \"who is your trusted grown-up for moments like this?\" don't tap it. stop, and show a trusted grown-up. surprise prizes online are often tricks. s4.1 s2.3 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-super-speed-cereal","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":5,"areas":["S2.0","S3.0"],"text":"super-speed cereal? a cartoon bunny says, \"crunchy stars cereal makes you run faster than a car!\" fact or fake? fact fake students show thumbs up or down, then tell a partner why. optional: show a teacher-made ad drawing on the projector. ask: \"is the bunny telling you something or selling you something?\" fake. it is an ad, and ads try to get you to buy. no cereal makes you run faster than a car. s2.2 s3.2 fact or fake daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy k-2"},{"id":"op:k2-say-whose-idea","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["K-2"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S3.0","AI2.0"],"text":"say whose idea mia drew a cat. leo put mia's cat on the class board and said, \"look what i made!\" was that fair? fair not fair students show thumbs up for fair or down for not fair. then pairs practice what leo could say instead, and ask: \"how would mia feel?\" not fair. leo should say, \"mia made this.\" we always say whose idea or picture it is, even if a computer helped. s3.2 ai2.3 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship k-2 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:35-finish-my-sentence","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S1.0","AI1.0"],"text":"finish my sentence finish these out loud: \"peanut butter and ___.\" \"once upon a ___.\" how did your brain know what comes next?  the class calls out endings together, then partners explain how they knew. ask: \"if a chatbot guesses the next word the same way, what could go wrong?\" you guessed from patterns you have heard many times. a chatbot also predicts likely next words from patterns, which is why it can sound right and still be wrong. s1.3 ai1.1 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 3-5 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works"},{"id":"op:35-confident-and-wrong","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":7,"areas":["S4.0","AI4.0","AI1.0"],"text":"confident and wrong? a chatbot says: \"lake marigold in pinecrest county is the deepest lake on earth. scientists agree.\" fact or fake, and how would you check? fact fake i need to check students hold up 1, 2, or 3 fingers, then write one way to check on a whiteboard. optional: you ask a district-approved chatbot a question on the projector and the class checks its answer against a trusted source. ask: \"does sounding sure make something true?\" fake: lake marigold and pinecrest county are made up for this opener. \"i need to check\" is the smart reflex; chatbots can state invented facts confidently, so we check a trusted source before we believe or repeat it. s4.1 ai4.1 ai1.3 fact or fake daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy media literacy 3-5 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:35-robot-smiley-face","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":10,"areas":["S5.0","AI1.0","S3.0"],"text":"draw exactly what i say write three instructions for drawing a smiley face. your partner is a robot who draws exactly what you say, nothing more.  partners sit back to back; one reads instructions and the other draws only what is said. swap, then ask: \"which words made the robot's drawing match, and which made it go weird?\"  s5.1 ai1.1 s3.2 draw daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 3-5 prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting"},{"id":"op:35-password-line-up","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","S5.0"],"text":"password line-up sort these practice passwords from weakest to strongest. none of these are anyone's real password. 123456 fluffy kai2016 purpletacojumpshigh purple-taco-jumps-high-7! write the five on the board; table groups agree on an order and send one person to rearrange them. ask: \"what makes the last one hard to guess but easy for you to remember?\" weakest to strongest: 123456, fluffy, kai2016, purpletacojumpshigh, purple-taco-jumps-high-7!. long, silly passphrases with symbols are strong, and names and birth years are easy to guess. never share your real one, except with a trusted grown-up. s2.1 s5.2 sort daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 3-5 scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:35-grandma-teacher-stranger","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0"],"text":"grandma, teacher, stranger ravi wants to post: \"home alone till 6! here's a pic of my front door on birch lane.\" stand up if you'd be ok with a stranger seeing that post.  students stand or stay seated; ask a sitter and a stander to explain. then ask: \"how could ravi change the post so it's safe to share?\" it shares when he is alone and where he lives. online posts can be copied and last a long time, so leave out location, schedule, and home details. s2.3 s4.3 stand up, sit down daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 3-5"},{"id":"op:35-ad-or-info-sort","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","S3.0"],"text":"ad or info? sort these into ad, information, or clickbait. \"ms. okafor's class guide: how to plant bean seeds\" \"try glowkicks sneakers, the ones all the cool kids wear!\" \"you won't believe what this cat did next!!!\" \"library hours change monday: open 9 to 5\" \"this video is sponsored by snack galaxy\" read each; students use corners of the room (ad, info, clickbait). ask: \"what words gave it away?\" info: the bean guide and library hours. ads: glowkicks and the sponsored video. clickbait: the cat headline, which uses shocking words to get a click. s2.2 s3.2 sort daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy 3-5 ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion"},{"id":"op:35-giant-fish-photo","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":7,"areas":["S5.0","S2.0","AI4.0"],"text":"the giant fish photo a post shows a fish bigger than a boat, caught at willow creek park: \"real photo, taken yesterday!\" before you share it, what are your steps?  describe the photo aloud, or show a teacher-made edited image on the projector. partners write two steps on a whiteboard, and then you ask: \"why is stop a better first move than staring really hard?\" stop first, then find better coverage: look for the same story from a trusted source. photos can be cropped, edited, or made with ai, so staring is not enough. s5.1 s2.2 ai4.1 image talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy generative ai literacy 3-5 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:35-credit-the-creator","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0","AI2.0"],"text":"credit the creator jada put a drawing by an artist named lu chen on her poster. rafael used an ai tool to brainstorm his poster's title. what should each of them do? nothing, it's fine say who made it or helped say they did it all themselves students hold up 1, 2, or 3 fingers. then pairs write the exact credit line jada and rafael could add. ask: \"why does an honest note matter, even for ai help?\" say who made it or helped. jada writes \"drawing by lu chen,\" and rafael adds \"i used ai to brainstorm title ideas and chose my own.\" s4.1 ai2.3 ai2.1 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship generative ai literacy 3-5"},{"id":"op:35-poster-tool-pick","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S3.0","S1.0"],"text":"screen or chart paper? your team is making a poster about recycling. would you rather make slides on devices, or one big chart-paper poster together? slides on devices one chart-paper poster students move to one side of the room and each side gives its best reason. ask: \"what does our team need to show, and which tool (or no tool) helps us show it?\"  s3.1 s1.1 s3.3 would you rather daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 3-5 data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards"},{"id":"op:35-kind-rewrite","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S3.0","S2.0"],"text":"rewrite it kind in a game chat, nico writes to sam: \"lol your video is so bad.\" rewrite it so it's honest, kind, and helpful.  students write one rewrite on a sticky note, then read it to a partner. ask: \"what changed, and how would sam feel now?\"  s3.2 s2.3 quick write daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 3-5 algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds"},{"id":"op:35-balance-goal","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0"],"text":"my balance goal think about your screen time and your green time this week. write one small goal you can check off by friday.  model one goal (\"i'll read outside for ten minutes twice\"), then students write theirs on an index card. ask: \"how will you know if you met it?\"  s4.2 s4.3 quick write daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 3-5 screen time screen time screentime screen-time screen use device use school-issued device hb 1481 media plan green time unplugged time screen time wellbeing and balance wellbeing well-being balance sleep companion chatbot emotional support wellbeing and balance"},{"id":"op:35-file-name-vote","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S1.0","S4.0"],"text":"which file name wins? you need to find your plant journal next month. which file name will help you most? untitled (3) asdfgh science-plant-journal-oct my stuff students hold up 1–4 fingers. then pairs invent a naming rule the class could use. ask: \"what should every file name tell you?\" science-plant-journal-oct, because it tells the subject, topic, and when. good names help you own and find your work. s1.1 s4.1 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 3-5"},{"id":"op:35-speaker-always-right","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S1.0","S2.0","AI1.0"],"text":"is the speaker always right? fact or fake: \"a smart speaker only turns on when you say its wake word, and its answers are always right.\" fact fake partly true students vote with thumbs up, down, or sideways for partly. ask: \"what should you never say near a speaker, and why?\" partly true. speakers listen for a wake word but can mishear and turn on by accident, may keep recordings, and answer by guessing, so keep private info away from them and check important answers. s1.3 s2.1 ai1.3 fact or fake daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy digital citizenship 3-5"},{"id":"op:35-lime-fruit-test","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S5.0","AI1.0"],"text":"is a lime a fruit? a sorting ai learned \"fruit is red or yellow\" from pictures of apples and bananas. stand up if you think it will call a lime a fruit.  students stand or sit, then two volunteers explain. ask: \"what training pictures would you add to fix the pattern?\" it probably won't, because its pattern only says red or yellow. ai learns from the examples it gets, so missing examples create mistakes and bias. s5.3 ai1.3 ai1.1 stand up, sit down daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 3-5 bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works"},{"id":"op:35-headline-from-2040","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["3-5"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S3.0","AI2.0","S5.0"],"text":"headline from 2040 pretend headline from the future: \"robot helpers now in every classroom in maple hollow.\" what's one good thing and one tricky thing?  partners each name one good and one tricky thing, then pair with another pair to compare. ask: \"did someone notice a problem you missed? what rule would fix it?\"  s3.3 ai2.2 s5.2 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 3-5"},{"id":"op:68-shark-on-main-street","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","AI4.0"],"text":"shark on main street? picture this photo, posted during a storm: a shark fin cuts through brown floodwater on main street in cedar bend, right past a stop sign. the caption says \"this is happening now. share before they delete it!\" what do you notice, what do you wonder, and what would you check before you believed it?  read the description twice, then give 60 seconds of silent think time before partners share one notice and one wonder. ask: \"would zooming in on the photo settle it, or do you need to check something outside the photo?\" you cannot tell from the image alone. old photos get reused, edits are easy, and ai can generate a scene like this in seconds. the strongest move is to leave the post and look for the source and for local news coverage of the storm. s2.2 ai4.1 image talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy generative ai literacy 6-8"},{"id":"op:68-the-lake-that-isnt-there","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S1.0","AI1.0"],"text":"the lake that isn't there a student asked a chatbot for help with a geography project. it answered: \"lake pembrook is the deepest lake in texas, at 912 feet. it was formed by a meteor about 10,000 years ago.\" fact or fake? fact fake can't tell yet students show one finger for fact, two for fake, three for can't tell yet. call on one student from each group to explain, then ask: \"what would you do next if this showed up in your notes?\" fake. lake pembrook is invented for this opener, and so are the depth and the meteor story. a chatbot predicts likely-sounding words, so it can state made-up details with total confidence. \"can't tell yet\" is a smart first answer; the fix is to check an atlas or a trusted source before you use it. s1.3 ai1.3 fact or fake daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 6-8 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works"},{"id":"op:68-shortcut-or-study-help","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S4.0","AI3.0"],"text":"shortcut or study help? sort these five ai uses into \"builds my learning\" or \"replaces my learning\": 1) asking ai to quiz me on vocabulary, 2) pasting in my essay question and turning in the answer, 3) asking ai to explain fractions a different way, 4) having ai write my reading log, 5) asking ai what i might have missed in my science notes. builds my learning replaces my learning depends how i use it students jot the numbers under two or three headings on a whiteboard, then compare with a partner. ask: \"pick one that depends. what would make it build learning instead of replace it?\" uses 1, 3, and 5 keep you doing the thinking, so they usually build learning. uses 2 and 4 hand the thinking to the tool. any of them can shift: even a quiz stops helping if you just copy answers, which is why your teacher's rules and your honesty both matter. s4.1 ai3.1 sort daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 6-8 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:68-free-gems-message","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S2.0"],"text":"free gems, just log in a message pops up in your game chat from \"mod_zyra\": \"congrats! you won 5,000 free gems for being a top player. log in at gemdrop-rewards.co in the next 10 minutes or they expire.\" what do you do? click and claim it ignore and block check another way first students move to three corners of the room, one for each option. each corner names the clue that decided it for them. ask: \"if this message had zero spelling mistakes, would that make it safe?\" treat it as a scam. the countdown, the prize you never entered for, and a login link outside the game are the real clues. perfect spelling proves nothing because ai can polish scam messages. if you want to check, open the game yourself, never through the link, and tell a trusted adult. s2.1 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 6-8 scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security"},{"id":"op:68-location-on-or-off","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0"],"text":"location: always, sometimes, never? would you rather share your live location with your friends all the time so it is easy to meet up, or keep it off and text people where you are when you need to? either way, who else might be able to see where you go? share with friends all the time keep it off and text instead students stand on one side of the room or the other, then talk with someone from the opposite side for one minute. ask: \"is there a middle choice, like sharing only with family or only during a trip?\" there is no single right answer. sharing is convenient and can help family keep you safe, but a trail of locations can reveal your home, school, and routine to more people than you meant. many apps let you share with a short list or for a set time. s2.1 s4.1 would you rather daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 6-8"},{"id":"op:68-headline-meets-lede","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S2.0","S3.0"],"text":"headline meets lede headline: \"homework banned in fernwood schools!\" first sentence of the article: \"a fernwood district committee will spend the next year studying how much homework students get and may suggest changes.\" does the headline match the story? matches stretches it contradicts it students hold up one, two, or three fingers. partners then rewrite the headline in eight words or fewer so it matches the lede. ask: \"why would someone write the first headline?\" it stretches it, almost to a contradiction. nothing is banned; a committee is only studying the question. dramatic headlines get more clicks and shares, which is why reading past the headline matters. s2.2 s3.2 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy 6-8 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking ads and persuasion advertis sponsored clickbait persuasion vendor claim marketing ads and persuasion"},{"id":"op:68-draw-the-feed-rule","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S5.0","AI1.0"],"text":"draw the feed's rule nico watched one skateboarding video all the way to the end. now his feed is almost all skateboarding. draw the rule the app might be following as a flowchart or an \"if ... then ...\" sentence.  students sketch on scrap paper or whiteboards for three minutes, then compare with a partner. ask: \"what would nico have to do to break the pattern, and what might he never see now?\" a simple version: if a person watches a video to the end, then show more videos like it. real feeds use many signals, but the idea is the same: the app predicts what will keep you watching, which can narrow what you see. s5.3 ai1.1 draw daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy generative ai literacy 6-8 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards futures thinking futures wheel future headline pre-mortem backcast signal futures thinking"},{"id":"op:68-who-wrote-the-script","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S3.0","AI2.0"],"text":"who wrote the script? your group is making a two-minute video about a problem at school. one teammate says, \"let's just have ai write the whole script. it'll be done in a minute.\" another says that feels like cheating. how should your team decide?  partners talk for three minutes, then each pair shares one rule their team would agree on. ask: \"which parts of this project should stay your team's own ideas?\" strong answers split the job: the team picks the problem and the message, ai might help brainstorm or give feedback if the teacher allows it, and the team checks and rewrites anything it keeps and says how ai helped. s3.1 ai2.1 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 6-8 academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds families and community family night families parent community take-home families and community"},{"id":"op:68-picture-a-scientist","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S3.0","AI1.0"],"text":"picture a scientist a teacher asked an image generator for \"a scientist at work\" four times. every picture showed an older man with white hair and a lab coat, standing in a lab. what do you notice? who is missing?  students list who is missing on a whiteboard (for example, women, young scientists, people who work outdoors or with computers), then pairs rewrite the prompt to get a fuller picture. ask: \"where might the tool have learned this pattern?\" image tools learn from huge piles of existing pictures and captions, so they can repeat old stereotypes. scientists are all kinds of people doing all kinds of work. a more specific prompt can help, but noticing the pattern is the first step. s3.3 ai1.3 image talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy media literacy 6-8 bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:68-rewrite-the-roast","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S3.0"],"text":"rewrite the roast a classmate posts their project slideshow in the class forum. someone comments: \"lol this is so boring, did a 3rd grader make this?\" rewrite the comment so it is honest, kind, and actually helpful.  students write for two minutes, then swap with a partner who checks for one specific suggestion. ask: \"what makes feedback useful instead of just nice or just mean?\" a helpful rewrite names something that works, one specific change, and a reason, such as \"your topic is interesting. maybe add a photo on slide 3 so the example stands out.\" s3.2 s3.1 quick write daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 6-8 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds"},{"id":"op:68-one-screen-goal","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0"],"text":"one screen goal think about yesterday. when did a screen help you, and when did it take time you wanted for something else? write one small, realistic screen goal for this week and how you will know you met it.  students write privately for three minutes; share only if they choose. ask volunteers: \"what makes a goal realistic instead of too big?\"  s4.2 s4.3 quick write daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 6-8"},{"id":"op:68-stand-if-you-check","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0","AI4.0"],"text":"stand if you check stand up if the statement is true for you, then sit back down. 1) i have believed something online that turned out to be false. 2) i have checked a second source before sharing. 3) i have used an ai answer without checking it. 4) i know one habit i want to get better at.  read each statement and pause for students to stand. no one explains unless they volunteer. finish by asking students to whisper to a partner the one checking habit they want to practice.  s4.3 ai4.1 stand up, sit down daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy media literacy 6-8"},{"id":"op:68-prompt-glow-up","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"hybrid","minutes":10,"areas":["S1.0","AI3.0"],"text":"prompt glow-up here is a weak prompt: \"tell me about volcanoes.\" with a partner, rewrite it so an ai would help you study for friday's quiz on how volcanoes form, without just doing the work for you.  pairs rewrite the prompt for three minutes and share a few. then you type the original and one student prompt into a district-approved chatbot on the projector (students do not use their own accounts). ask: \"which answer helps you learn, and what would you still need to check?\" stronger prompts say who you are, what you need, and how you want help, for example: \"i'm in 7th grade. quiz me with five questions about how volcanoes form and explain any i get wrong.\" even a good answer still needs checking against your notes or textbook. s1.1 s1.3 ai3.1 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 6-8 how ai works next word next-word predict pattern training data language model how ai works prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting"},{"id":"op:68-landslide-chart","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S5.0"],"text":"the landslide chart picture a bar chart titled \"tacos win by a landslide!\" the taco bar towers over the pizza bar. but look closely at the side: the numbers start at 48, not 0. tacos got 52 votes and pizza got 49. is \"landslide\" fair? fair misleading need more info students vote with fingers, then sketch the same chart with the axis starting at 0. ask: \"why might someone start the axis at 48?\" misleading. the numbers in this mock chart are close (52 to 49), but starting the axis at 48 makes the gap look huge. always check where the axis starts before you trust how big a difference looks. s5.1 s5.3 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy 6-8 data and dashboards dashboard data team chart student data data snapshot data and dashboards"},{"id":"op:68-remix-it-right","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["6-8"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","S5.0"],"text":"remix it right sort each remix: 1) using a song you found online as background music in a video you post publicly, 2) using a photo marked with a creative commons license and crediting the creator, 3) drawing your own version of a cartoon character for your notebook, 4) posting an ai-made image as if you drew it. okay as is okay with credit or permission not okay students sort the numbers on a whiteboard and compare in groups of three. ask: \"what changes when you post something publicly instead of keeping it for yourself?\" 1 needs permission or music you are allowed to use. 2 is okay because the license allows it and you gave credit. 3 is usually fine for personal practice. 4 is not okay; be honest that ai made it. s2.3 s5.2 sort daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship generative ai literacy 6-8 copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:912-citation-to-nowhere","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","AI4.0"],"text":"the citation to nowhere an ai research brief says: \"teens who study with music score 40% higher on memory tests (harlow & min, 2021, journal of adolescent learning science, vol. 14).\" it looks polished and specific. fact or fake? fact fake can't tell until i trace it students vote with fingers, then pairs list the exact steps they would take to trace the citation. ask: \"what would count as proof that this study exists and says this?\" treat it as fake until traced. the authors, journal, and 40% figure were invented for this opener, which is exactly what a hallucinated citation looks like: real-sounding names, a volume number, a neat statistic. search for the article itself, confirm the journal exists, and read what the study actually found. s2.2 ai4.2 fact or fake daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy media literacy 9-12 higher ed copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit ai hallucinations hallucinat confidently wrong fabricated made-up citation verify verification ai hallucinations"},{"id":"op:912-the-voicemail-from-dana","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["medialit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","AI2.0"],"text":"the voicemail from dana the night before the riverton mayoral election, a voicemail spreads in group chats. it sounds exactly like candidate dana whitfield: \"because of the storm, voting for my supporters has moved to wednesday.\" your cousin asks if they should forward it. forward it don't forward it check first, then decide students go to three corners and each corner explains its reasoning in one sentence. ask: \"where would you go to confirm when and where voting happens?\" don't forward it, and check the official election office. a familiar voice is no longer proof, since voices can be cloned from short clips. election dates don't move for one candidate's supporters, and the timing (the night before) is a classic pressure tactic. s2.2 ai2.2 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy generative ai literacy 9-12 deepfakes deepfake synthetic media synthetic audio voice clone cloned voice cheapfake ai-generated image manipulated video deepfakes kindness and cyberbullying cyberbullying upstander kind words unkind group chat respectful communication kindness and cyberbullying"},{"id":"op:912-one-honest-sentence","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0","AI2.0"],"text":"one honest sentence jaylen used an ai chatbot to brainstorm three possible thesis statements for a history essay, picked one, rewrote it, and wrote the whole essay himself. his teacher allows ai for brainstorming if you disclose it. write the one-sentence disclosure jaylen should put at the end.  students write for two minutes, then trade with a partner who checks: does it say what tool type, what it did, and what jaylen did himself? ask: \"what would make a disclosure feel honest instead of like a loophole?\" a strong example: \"i used an ai chatbot to brainstorm three possible thesis statements; i chose and revised one, and the research, argument, and writing are my own.\" s4.1 ai2.3 quick write daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy digital citizenship 9-12 higher ed academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity"},{"id":"op:912-free-app-or-private-app","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["digcit","genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","AI2.0"],"text":"free app or private app? would you rather use studynest ai, which is free but reads every document in your cloud drive to \"personalize your help,\" or quietnotes, which costs a few dollars a month and says it deletes your files after each session? studynest ai (free, reads everything) quietnotes (paid, deletes files) students move to one side of the room, then talk across the line with someone who chose differently. ask: \"what would you need to read or change before trusting either one?\" both are fictional, and both claims need checking. free tools often pay for themselves with your data, while a paid tool's promise is only as good as its privacy policy. good questions: what is collected, who it is shared with, how long it is kept, and whether you can turn it off or delete it. s2.1 ai2.2 would you rather daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship generative ai literacy 9-12 higher ed privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:912-solevault-storefront","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["digcit","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S5.0","S2.0"],"text":"the solevault storefront picture an online store called solevault outlet: limited sneakers at 80% off, a timer counting down from 9:59, hundreds of five-star reviews all posted the same week, and a checkout page that only accepts gift cards or a payment app transfer. what do you notice, and what would you check, in order?  pairs list the red flags, then put their checks in the order they would do them. ask: \"which single clue would make you walk away, even if everything else looked fine?\" the biggest clue is the payment method: gift cards and direct transfers are hard or impossible to reverse. the countdown creates pressure, the too-good price is a lure, and a burst of same-week reviews can be fake or ai-written. a careful order: look up the store's name plus \"scam\" or \"reviews,\" check its contact and return info, and pay only with a method that offers protection. s5.1 s2.1 image talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship media literacy 9-12 higher ed scams and security phishing scam password passphrase two-step cybersecurity malware hacked scams and security"},{"id":"op:912-redesign-one-feature","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["medialit","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":10,"areas":["S5.0"],"text":"redesign one feature pick one feature of a social app: infinite scroll, autoplay, streaks, or like counts. with a partner, explain what it is designed to make you do, then redesign it so it serves people instead of engagement.  pairs choose one feature, talk for four minutes, and sketch the redesign in one sentence or a quick drawing. share three. ask: \"would the company that makes the app want your version? why or why not?\" examples: scroll that pauses with a \"you're caught up\" message, autoplay that is off by default, streaks that can be paused without penalty, like counts only the poster sees. the tension is that these redesigns may reduce time on the app, which is what many apps are built to increase. s5.2 s5.1 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy digital citizenship 9-12 algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds"},{"id":"op:912-who-owns-the-winner","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S3.0","AI2.0"],"text":"who owns the winner? priya typed a detailed prompt into an image generator, picked the best of 30 results, edited the colors, and won the lakeview art contest. who deserves the most credit for the image? priya the company that made the tool the artists whose work trained it no one fully students go to four corners, then each corner sends one speaker to make its best case in 30 seconds. ask: \"did anyone hear an argument from another corner that made them want to move?\" this is an open question; courts, contests, and schools are still working it out. strong answers weigh priya's choices, the tool's role, and the artists whose work trained the model, and they notice that the contest's own rules and honest disclosure matter a lot. s3.3 ai2.1 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy digital citizenship 9-12 higher ed academic integrity academic integrity disclosure disclose ai-use statement cheating detector process log academic integrity prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting"},{"id":"op:912-coach-or-crutch","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0","AI3.0"],"text":"coach or crutch? big chemistry test tomorrow. would you rather use an ai that gives you correct answers to the practice problems instantly, or one that only asks you questions and hints until you solve them yourself? instant answers questions and hints only students show a thumb up for instant answers, thumb down for hints, then write one line: which would actually help them on the test, and why. ask: \"what does your choice say about how you learn best?\" instant answers feel faster, but the test asks you to solve problems without help, so practicing the solving is what transfers. hints can be slower and more frustrating, which is often a sign your brain is doing the work. a mix can work: try first, then check. s4.3 ai3.1 would you rather daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 9-12 prompting prompt custom instructions instructions for a clear directions prompting ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:912-draft-the-rule","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["genai","digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":10,"areas":["S3.0","AI2.0"],"text":"draft the rule your school board asks students for one sentence to include in the new ai-use policy. with a partner, draft that sentence, then ask: who might be left out or hurt by it?  pairs draft for three minutes, then join another pair to test each sentence against \"who might be left out?\" collect the best sentences on the board. ask: \"what evidence would convince adults your rule works?\"  s3.2 s3.3 ai2.1 turn and talk daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy digital citizenship 9-12 ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:912-focusfuel-gummies","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0"],"text":"focusfuel gummies a fitness influencer, coach rhys, posts: \"focusfuel gummies boost your memory 300% before exams. doctors don't want you to know this. link in bio for 20% off!\" fact or fake? fact fake can't tell yet students vote with fingers, then pairs list what they would search to read laterally about coach rhys and focusfuel. ask: \"who makes money if you believe this?\" treat it as fake. focusfuel, coach rhys, and the 300% figure are invented for this opener, and the post shows classic warning signs: a huge number with no source, a \"doctors don't want you to know\" hook, and a discount link that pays the poster. reading laterally means leaving the post to see what independent, qualified sources say, and asking a trusted adult or clinician before taking any supplement. s2.2 s4.1 fact or fake daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now media literacy 9-12"},{"id":"op:912-the-fourth-teammate","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12","Higher Ed"],"strands":["genai"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S3.0","AI5.0"],"text":"the fourth teammate your team of three is making a short documentary, and ai is your \"fourth teammate.\" sort these jobs: brainstorm interview questions, choose whose story to tell, write captions for accessibility, decide the film's main message, suggest music, fact-check a statistic. ai can help humans only team decides together groups sort the jobs on a whiteboard in three columns, then compare with another group. ask: \"which job did you disagree about, and what would settle it?\" choosing whose story to tell and the main message are human judgment calls. ai can help brainstorm questions or draft captions that people then check. fact-checking can't be handed to ai alone because it can be wrong too; it can point you to where to look, but a person confirms with a source. s3.1 ai5.1 sort daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy 9-12 higher ed misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation bias and fairness bias stereotype fairness who is missing whose story representation bias and fairness accessibility and inclusion accessib screen reader udl inclusive emergent bilingual digital divide who gets left out accessibility and inclusion ai policy and guidance policy guidance statement acceptable use guardrail board ai policy and guidance"},{"id":"op:912-post-from-ninth-grade","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["digcit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S2.0","S4.0"],"text":"the post from ninth grade imagine a scholarship committee or a future boss searches your name. they find a joke you posted in ninth grade that made fun of someone. write what you would want them to understand, and one thing you would do now to shape what people find.  students write privately for three minutes; volunteers share only the second part. ask: \"is deleting enough? where else might that post still exist?\"  s2.3 s4.3 quick write daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship 9-12 privacy privacy personal information personal data data trail digital footprint tracking de-identif privacy"},{"id":"op:912-stand-for-the-streak","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["digcit","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":5,"areas":["S4.0"],"text":"stand for the streak stand if it's true for you, then sit. 1) i have opened an app just to keep a streak alive. 2) i have lost track of time on autoplay. 3) i have turned off a notification on purpose. 4) i have a time of day that's screen-free. then set one small goal for this week.  read each statement and pause; nobody explains unless they volunteer. close with a 60-second private write: one goal and how you'll know you met it. ask: \"which of these was designed by the app, and which was your choice?\"  s4.2 s4.3 stand up, sit down daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship media literacy 9-12"},{"id":"op:912-summary-that-drifted","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["genai","medialit"],"mode":"unplugged","minutes":7,"areas":["S1.0","AI4.0"],"text":"the summary that drifted from a fictional 1911 letter: \"the mill fire began after dark. mr. okafor and the ruiz family carried water until dawn. some say a lantern tipped, but no one saw it.\" an ai summary: \"the mill fire started at 6 p.m. when a lantern tipped over. the mayor led the rescue.\" which change matters most? the added time the rumor turned into fact the helpers who disappeared all of them students vote with fingers, then partners defend their pick. ask: \"if you only read the summary, what would you get wrong on a test or in a paper?\" all of them. the summary invents a specific time, turns an unconfirmed rumor into a cause, and replaces the neighbors who helped with a mayor who is not in the letter. ai summaries can save time, but you still have to compare them with the original. s1.3 ai4.1 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now generative ai literacy media literacy 9-12 misinformation misinformation disinformation rumor viral fake news fact-check lateral reading sift misinformation copyright and credit copyright fair use creative commons give credit citation cite remix intellectual property copyright and credit"},{"id":"op:912-what-the-ads-think","type":"opener","audience":"student","roles":[],"grades":["9-12"],"strands":["digcit","medialit"],"mode":"digital","minutes":10,"areas":["S1.0","S2.0"],"text":"what the ads think you like on a device and account you already use, find the ad settings or \"why am i seeing this ad\" page. look at the interests it has guessed about you. how accurate is the profile? creepy accurate mostly right way off couldn't find it give students four minutes to look; anyone without an account or device pairs up or watches you walk through a school demo account on the projector. students vote with fingers and share one surprising guess only if they want to. ask: \"what did you do online that might have led to that guess, and will you change any setting?\"  s1.1 s2.1 vote daily opener daily opener bellringer bell ringer warm up warm-up do now digital citizenship media literacy 9-12 algorithms and feeds algorithm feed recommendation engagement design scroll algorithms and feeds"}];
